pages.text:
Pioneer's is a tech mod played §lage by age§r: from the flint knife to bronze and steam, electricity, chemistry, the atom, then space, all the way to holding a black hole and simulating galaxies.

§6The world changes from the start§r:
• §lno ores§r underground anymore: metal is sifted, then prospected and drilled;
• tools are made at §lbenches§r, not in the crafting grid;
• metal melts in a §lcrucible§r, not in the furnace;
• water is only infinite in §lrivers and oceans§r;
• §lruins§r of a forgotten industry await, guarded.

§6Required§r: Minecraft 1.20.1, Forge 47.4 or later. §6Recommended§r: JEI. Back up your worlds before each update.

§6Keys§r (rebindable in Options > Controls):
• §lH§r: this guide (Ctrl + F: search);
• §lJ§r: the Field Notebook;
• §lK§r: the research tree;
• §lM§r: the system map (§lG§r: the galaxy, §lL§r: rocket logistics);
• §lN§r: the world map; §lY§r: toggle the minimap; §lI§r: a waypoint; §l=§r / §l-§r: zoom;
• §lO§r: the robotics logistics map (Shift + O while aiming at a block);
• §lG§r: modules of the gear you wear;
• §lU§r: clan territory borders;
• §lB§r / §lV§r: capsule parachute and autopilot;
• §lL§r (Minecraft): advancements, which follow the ages.

Handy gestures (wrench, multiblock blueprints, look cards, cables): next page.

§6To do§r
Build the compact steam machines (grid) and feed them through the bronze cable (3 mB of steam per tick each while working). They are the §ltier 1§r machines: each one also makes the mechanical recipes of its family (tier 0), §ltwice as fast§r and §l×1.5§r on raw materials:
• §lmacerator§r: 1 raw ore → 3 crushed (the millstone and the jaw crusher make 2);
• §lfurnace§r, §lcompactor§r, §lextractor§r, §lrolling mill§r (ingot → plate), §lwiremill§r (ingot → wires), §lsaw§r (log → 9 planks, stone cutting);
• §lsteam press§r (it flattens: ingot + die → plate), §lsteam sifter§r (the shaking sifter); later, with steel rods, the §lsteam lathe§r (steel rods, bolts, screws, rings, springs).

§6Why§r: everything you did by hand now runs by itself.

§6To do§r
1. Metalworker (hammer, file): a §lwrench§r (brass: brass plates and rod + steel screw; steel: steel plates, rod and screw). It is the tool of every industrial bench.
2. Grid:
• §lbasic components bench§r, in iron: 2 plates, 1 gear, 4 iron rods, 1 log. Shapes by hand: plates, rods, wires, screws, bolts, springs, gears, rings;
• §lassembly bench§r: plates, wrench, rods, the metalworker's bench, steel ingots. It only makes machine hulls, machines and multiblock parts;
• §ladvanced components bench§r: plates, wrench, copper wires, the basic components bench, rods: frames, motors, circuits, bearings, pumps;
• §llogistics parts bench§r (later): rail tracks and posts, harbors, rockets, robotics (vehicles: railroad works and shipyard).

§6How§r: wrench in a tool slot, parts in the 6 slots, pick from the list (doable ones first), “Craft” or “× n”.

§6Alongside§r petrochemistry:
• industrial crops (rapeseed, hemp, corn): oils, fibers, starch;
• crusher (chips), compactor (pellets), fermenter (anaerobic digestion: biogas);
• extractor (oil pressing) (lubricant, biodiesel), pyrolysis oven (biochar);
• §lhydroponic farm§r, plantation, aquaculture: mass production.

§6Why§r: renewable fuels and materials, and alternative recipes.

No more sub-crafts in the grid. Each §6workshop§r has its tools (up to 3) and materials (6 slots): the list shows §leverything it can make§r, lit up when possible. Pick the part, then §lMake§r (or ×N). Tools lose one point per part. §lRule§r (1.0.5): each item is made by the trade of §lits material§r (leather and textiles at the tanner, wood at the carpenter, metal at the metalworker or coppersmith, stone at the mason, clay and glass at the potter, crops at the farming bench).
• §ltoolmaker§r: tools (head + handle + lashing), knapped flint;
• §lcarpenter§r (saw, knife, hammer): planks, sticks, laths, pegs, glues; stairs, slabs, fences, doors, trapdoors, signs, chests, barrels, ladders, beds, bookshelves; §lwooden boats§r, chest boats and rafts (1.0.5);
• §lmetalworker§r (hammer, file, saw): bolts and screws cut from a rod, wrenches, machined parts, and the §larmory§r: metal armor and horse armor, musket, revolver, lead balls and cartridges; plates, rods and gears are cast, wire and tool heads are forged;
• §lpotter§r (knife, file): unfired molds, bricks, crucibles; glass: panes, stained glass, bottles, lenses, mirrors, aquarium; flower pots;
• §lmason§r (mortar, hammer): gravel, sand, flint dust, fire clay; stone stairs, slabs and walls, bricks, cut stone, blast furnace, concrete powder;
• §lcoppersmith§r: conduits, boilers, fittings, dials; bucket, cauldron, pots; cut copper;
• §ltanner§r (knife, shears): tanned leather, leather and rubber gaskets and belts, fibers, thread, canvas, ropes, lead, saddle, pack saddle, bags and outfits;
• §lredstoner§r: pistons, dispensers, observers, repeaters, comparators, torches, lamps, pressure plates;
• §ltransporter§r: tracks, signals, railroad crossings (wooden boats: carpenter and shipyard; minecarts: railroad works);
• §lluthier§r (saw, knife, file): flute, guitar, accordion, drum, note block, jukebox;
• §lfarming§r (saw, knife, hammer, mortar): wooden kitchen stations, compost bin, scarecrow, apiary, incubator, nesting box, fishing net, crab pot and rod, hay bale; flours and mortar milling, bone meal.
The grid is only for assemblies now (and planks, sticks, crafting table, furnace, campfire, torches).
§lMachine only§r (1.0.5: steam machines, tier 1, and up), never at a bench: natural rubber and resin glue (extractor), coal ball (compressor), steel sheet (rolling mill), steel screws, bolts and balls, honed cylinder (lathe), perforated board (press), copper coil (wiremill), cast iron housings (induction furnace).

Five §6workshop tools§r: §lhammer, file, saw, knife, mortar§r. They no longer go in the grid: you set them on a §6workshop§r (toolmaker, carpenter, metalworker, potter, mason, coppersmith — see the “Workshops” page), and they lose one point per part.
• §lhammer§r: crushed ore, and forging at the anvil;
• §lfile§r: rods, screws;
• §lsaw§r: bolts, planks;
• §lknife§r: plant fibers, ropes, canvas;
• §lmortar§r: dusts, flour, flint and bone dust.
The first ones are stone and flint, then metal §lforged§r at the anvil (“Mechanics: Foundry and forge” chapter). Each metal has its range of parts (§lingot, nugget, plate, rod, bolt, screw, ring, gear, spring, dust§r), under forge tags.

At the §6toolmaker§r, no tool needed: §l1 or 2 flint§r → a head (knife blade, pickaxe, axe, shovel or hoe head, saw blade). Then §lhead + stick(s) + a binding§r (fiber, string or leather) → the tool.
• §lknife§r: plant fibers (tall grass, vines); it is also the workshop tool for ropes and canvas;
• §lpickaxe§r: stone, coal, fired clay;
• §laxe§r: fells the §lwhole tree§r at once (connected logs, natural leaves; a log cabin does not fall); sneak for a single log; every log wears the axe;
• §lshovel, hoe, saw§r (the saw makes planks and bolts at the workshop).
Flint wears out fast: §lcopper§r then §lbronze§r tools are cast or forged next.

Three tools work over an §larea§r, in every metal (not in flint):
• the §6mining hammer§r breaks rock §l3 × 3§r, in the plane of the targeted face (same mining level as the pickaxe, but slower);
• the §6excavator§r digs soil, sand and gravel §l3 × 3§r; right-click on grass or dirt: a 3 × 3 §lpath§r;
• the §6scythe§r §lharvests and replants§r every ripe crop in a square — §l3 × 3§r at tiers 0-1, §l5 × 5§r at tiers 2-3, §l7 × 7§r at tiers 4-5 —, genetic crops included (each one restarts with its first offspring seed). It also mows grass and flowers, prunes leaves, sweeps enemies like a sword and §ltills§r the same square on right-click.
A neighbor only goes if the tool mines it and it is not much harder than the targeted block; chests and machines stay put. §lSneak§r: a single block. An outline shows the area, and cracks spread over the whole area while you mine.
Each block wears the tool by one point: hammer and excavator last 4 times longer than a pickaxe of the same metal, the scythe 3 times. Heads are forged on the anvil (§l5§r ingots for the hammer head, §l3§r for the bucket, §l2§r for the blade), in the forge press, machined or sintered like the others; assembled at the §6toolmaker§r with a long handle.

§6Hand sieve§r: fit a §lfiber mesh§r (512 passes), pour in gravel or sand (up to 16), shake (repeated empty-handed clicks). Nonstop: the Mechanical Age §6shaking sifter§r.

Where to look (each product has its own chance on every pass):
• §lgravel§r: copper and iron — malachite 75%, magnetite 60%, hematite 40%, flint 10%, gold 2%;
• §lsand§r: tin and copper — malachite 70%, cassiterite 50%, clay 60%, gold 2%;
• §6black sand§r on §lbeaches and oceans§r: tin and iron — cassiterite 70%, hematite 55%, magnetite 30%, zircon 8%, gold 4%;
• §6alluvial gravel§r in §lriver beds§r, or §lcrushed tuff§r (mason with a hammer, crushers, grinders): copper and tin — malachite 80%, cassiterite 65%, clay 40%, gold flakes 30%;
• red sand (hematite), dirt (seeds, clay).
§l4 fragments§r of a mineral make 1 raw ore (magnetite and hematite → iron, malachite → copper, cassiterite → tin); 9 flakes, a gold nugget. The fiber mesh lasts 512 passes.

§lQuench§r: the hot part thrown into water (water block, filled cauldron, quench basin) becomes the finished part, with a hiss and steam. Air-cooled, it stays rough: reheat it.
Everything can be automated before industry:
• §6quench basin§r: filled with water by bucket or pipe (4,000 mB, 25 mB per quench), it quenches in 3 s (steam, the part dims in the water) whatever you place with right-click, throw in or feed by hopper; take the finished part with an empty hand or let it out §lthe bottom§r. Empty hand on a busy basin: water left and seconds to go;
• the §6forge hearth§r takes hoppers: ingots from the top, charcoal from the side, heated ingots out the bottom, within the limits set in its screen;
• §6crucible furnace§r: automatic pouring into the casting table placed against it on any side (mold loaded by hopper).
§lIndustrial Age§r: the casting machine and the industrial casting machine (continuous casting) take §6steel dies§r (ingot, plate, rod, gear), made once and kept in the machine's §lTool slot§r (wear: 1 casting in 100); the §6industrial power hammer (hot forging)§r forges tool heads continuously, quench included.

Put several metals in the crucible: the screen shows the mix, and at the bottom §lwhat will pour and what will be left§r. Each alloy has its ideal ratio, in the middle of its range:
• §6bronze§r: 3 copper to 1 tin (copper 70–80%, tin 20–30%) — tools, gears, machines;
• §6brass§r: 3 copper to 1 zinc (copper 70–80%, zinc 20–30%) — fittings, dials, whistles;
• §6solder§r: 3 tin to 2 lead (tin 55–65%, lead 35–45%) — conduit joints.
§lNo bath ever gets stuck§r: if the mix is not exactly an alloy, the forge pours the §llargest share of alloy§r it can at the ideal ratio (with no choice: the alloy that uses the most of the bath) and keeps the surplus, which then pours as a pure metal or another alloy. At the §6crucible furnace§r, the §lRecipe§r slot is a §lfilter§r: it only takes the alloy's metals, in the right ratio (“waits for tin” on the screen), and only pours that alloy; what is not needed stays in its slot. The §lEmpty§r button as a last resort: the bath comes back as ingots and nuggets.

Steam is a §lgas§r: it travels in the §6logistic cable§r, like every gas (there are no separate pipes). In the Bronze Age, the §6bronze cable§r (coppersmith: 2 bronze plates, 1 bronze ring → 4); later the MKI to MKIV cables, faster.
The §6steam dome§r §lpushes§r its steam into the cables touching it; the cable delivers it to machines, filling their §lreserve§r. A machine touching the dome also takes it directly. §lRight-click§r the cable: the network diagram (who gives, who receives); §6wrench§r: cut or restore the aimed side.
• §6boiler low-water cutoff§r: placed on the boiler, it §lshuts the firebox down§r when the water drops below 20% (wrench: 5 to 50%) and restarts it at 25%: no more dry firing;
• §6steam gauge§r, §6water gauge§r: reserve and water level, comparator output;
• §6whistle§r: redstone, or a low-water alarm (the real danger).
These fittings go on the boiler or on a steam machine.

Twelve processing machines, all steam-driven (from the back, sides or below); each one uses §l3 mB of steam per tick§r while working (the induction furnace: 6 mB). They are the §ltier 1§r machines of their family (next page):
• §6macerator§r: the millstone and the jaw crusher (raw ore → §l3 crushed§r, crushed → dust, stone → gravel → sand), ingot → dust, flour, and §lredstone§r ore from the drill → 4 dust (redstone only comes from drills);
• §6sifter§r: the shaking sifter (gravel, sand, black sand, alluvial gravel); §6saw§r: the mechanical saw (log → §l9 planks§r), sticks and the mason's stone cutting; §6lathe§r: the wood lathe and steel (rods, rings, springs, bolts, screws, balls, honed cylinder);
• §6furnace§r (all smelting, not metal), §6compactor§r (coal ball, §l4 unfired fire bricks for 3 fire clay§r), §6extractor§r (natural rubber from latex, resin glue and rosin from resin), §6rolling mill§r (plates, and 2 sheets per plate), §6wiremill§r (wire, copper coils: 6 wires → 1), §6press§r (it flattens: ingot + plate die → plate, copper foil, plywood, perforated boards), §6mixer§r (concrete, the mason's mixes, §lfire clay ×2 to ×3§r: 2 clay + sand + flint + water → 4), §6induction furnace§r (direct alloys, next page).
What is §lmachine only§r (never at a bench) starts here: rubber, glue, coal ball, steel sheet and fasteners, balls, honed cylinder, perforated board, copper coil, red alloys: every key MKI component needs at least one.
Interface: steam gauge, yield, recipe §lselector§r, §lsides§r tab (items, steam) and §lejection§r; what no longer fits in the outputs waits inside the machine. In the Industrial Age, the family's MKI machine (and its multiblock) makes all these recipes, faster.

The §6steam induction furnace§r (tier 1, §lInduction§r family) makes §ldirect alloys§r: up to §l3 solid inputs → 1 solid item§r, with no bath and no casting. It uses §l6 mB of steam per tick§r (twice the other steam machines). Recipes:
• §6bronze§r 3 copper + 1 tin → 4 ingots; §6brass§r 3 copper + 1 zinc → 4; §6solder§r 3 tin + 2 lead → 5;
• §6red alloy§r 1 copper + 2 redstone dust → 1 ingot; §6redsteel§r 2 steel + 4 redstone dust + 1 §6coal ball§r (4 coal at the compactor) → 2 ingots; their dusts make an ingot again;
• §6cast iron housings§r and the pump housing (pig iron + zircon or sand).
Nothing is created: an alloy weighs what its metals weigh. The §6MKI to MKIV induction furnaces§r (tiers 2 to 5) make the same recipes, faster, plus constantan, invar, netherite, magnets, electrical steel sheet, carbide tools, zamak parts… Red alloy and redsteel do not melt: they are recycled as dust. What melts (ore, babbitt, duralumin, electrum…) stays in the §6industrial crucible§r: 3 solid inputs (+ one gas) → a molten metal and a little §lslag§r.

When the day comes, the ruin becomes the §lreal multiblock§r of its tier again, in working order. Then:
• a §6Restoration Plaque§r bears the names of those who filled the reserve;
• an entry joins the Codex, and a “First Awakening” advancement per tier;
• the §lnearest village§r, within 400 blocks, gains a §lservice§r depending on the machine: Water, Energy, Workshop, Warehouse or Archives (“Trade and villages” chapter);
• the Restorers think well of you (+20), the Bare Hands much less (−15); nearby villages gain +10 affinity toward each contributor (−10 among the Bare Hands).
The restored machine serves everyone; a server setting (ruins.restoredOwnerOnly) keeps it for those who raised it.

A metal is §learned§r: from ore to finished part it goes through a series of §lstages§r, each with its machines:
• raw §lore§r (deposits, drill) → §lcrushed§r (§6crusher§r) → §lground§r (§6crusher (fine grinding)§r, with water);
• §lconcentrate§r: §6flotation§r, §6ore separator (magnetic separation)§r, §6ore separator (shaking table)§r, §6ore separator (optical sorting)§r, screen; the rest goes to §ltailings§r;
• §ltreatment§r: roasting, leaching, chlorination, pellets or bales in the §6compactor§r;
• §lmetal§r: reduction, smelting (crucible, blast furnace, arc furnace), electrolysis; the result is often pig iron, a sponge, a ferroalloy or an impure metal;
• §lrefined§r: converter, electrorefining, vacuum remelting: the pure metal;
• §lsemi-finished§r (MKII): slab, billet, beam blank, wire rod, gear blank;
• §lshape§r: plate, rod, wire, sheet, ring, spring, gear, beam, tube, fasteners.
An ingot is shaped in §la single operation§r only by hand (the §6basic components bench§r, with losses) or, for the bench metals, along the routes of the page “Metal parts”. A simple route skips stages, a very complex one adds some; JEI shows the §ltier§r of every step (colored badge).

Each metal has §l1 to 5 routes§r; pick one from what you have and what you want:
• §lsimple§r (1 to 4 steps, compact machines): quick to set up, but it yields little, costs energy or reagents, leaves worthless waste and often gives an impure metal or a ferroalloy. Exception: the tier 1 metals (iron, copper, tin, lead, zinc, gold) have yields of their own (page “Ore yields”): §6crusher§r → §6industrial crucible§r → §6casting machine§r, §l3 ingots per ore at MKI, 6 at MKIV§r (§lcrushing doubles the metal§r);
• §lcomplex§r (5 to 8 steps): the right balance, with §lbyproducts§r that feed other chains: sulfuric acid from roasting, anode slime, Thomas slag, blast furnace gas;
• §lvery complex§r (8 to 12 steps, often at the next tier): the best yield, the purest metal (required by some components) and valuable byproducts;
• §lrecycling§r: scrap and offcuts compacted into bales (they melt at 95%, twice as fast), tailings leached again, worn carbide.
A metal that §lopens a tier§r (aluminum for MKII, titanium for MKIII, tungsten for MKIV) has a §lfirst route§r, the mastery test, which the machines of the tier before can run; its simple routes only come later.
At each machine tier, ore yields more (x1, x1.25, x1.5, x2); the §lMiner-Smelter§r gets another 10% on the ore steps of their machines. No metal of the Industrial Age smelts in a furnace or an industrial furnace: it needs one of its routes (only tier 1 metal ore smelts in the industrial furnace: one ingot per ore).

The tier 1 metals (§liron, copper, tin, lead, zinc, gold§r) follow one rule: §l1 ingot = 144 mB§r. Ingots per raw ore:
• §6industrial furnace§r: §l1§r; crushed first (§l2 crushed ores§r): §l2§r, at every tier;
• §6industrial crucible§r then §6casting machine§r: raw ore §l1.5 / 2 / 2.5 / 3§r (MKI / MKII / MKIII / MKIV); crushed: §l3 / 4 / 5 / 6§r;
• from MKII, §lacid leaching§r: §6extractor (leaching)§r (250 mB of sulfuric acid per crushed ore) → §6electrolyzer§r → dust → furnace: §l5 / 6 / 7§r, one ingot more than the crucible at the same tier.
The crucible multiplier only applies to §lore§r (raw, crushed), in the compact machine and in the multiblock of the same tier; ingots, dusts and nuggets stay ×1, a fragment melts into 36 mB. Clay crucible, crucible furnace and automatic foundry: ×1. §lCrushing doubles the metal§r: the jaw crusher, the steam crusher and the crusher all give 2 crushed ores. A remelted part (plate, rod, gear…) only gives back §l50%§r of its metal. JEI: page “Ore yields”.

The §lMKII§r metals:
• §lmagnesium§r: Pidgeon process (magnesia from brine and ferrosilicon in the §6industrial crucible (vacuum)§r, no electrolysis); dehydrated bischofite and molten chloride electrolysis (already at MKI: it goes into duralumin; the chloride from the Kroll goes back through it);
• §lzirconium§r: fused zirconia in the arc furnace (no metal, but the refractory of ceramic shell molds); chlorination and the §6Kroll reactor§r (alloy-grade zirconium); zirconium-hafnium separation (nuclear-grade zirconium for zircaloy, and hafnium oxide);
• §lniobium§r: ferroniobium by aluminothermy (high-strength steels); hydrofluoric attack, solvent extraction and aluminothermy (niobium metal); recycled fluorides and electron beam melting (MKIV: superconducting niobium);
• §lplatinum§r: it comes from §lbyproducts§r (copper anode slime, platinum-group concentrate from nickel): sponge from aqua regia (fast, impure); calcined ammonium chloroplatinate (pure); full separation (MKIII: platinum, osmium and iridium from one stream).

§lSilicon§r has five routes, one per generation of electronics:
• §lmetallurgical§r: quartz and coke in the §6arc furnace§r (98%: alloys and ferrosilicon, no electronics);
• §lSiemens deposition§r: trichlorosilane from a fluidized bed, distilled, deposited in the autoclave (the polysilicon of solar cells and Gen 2 chips);
• §lCzochralski§r: polysilicon pulled into a single-crystal ingot, sawn into wafers (Gen 2 and Gen 3);
• §lfloat zone§r (MKIII): hydrofluoric acid leaching, then float-zone refining under vacuum (no chlorine or hydrogen, but slow);
• §lrecycling§r reject wafers (MKIII, fast).

An alloy is §lnever made by mixing powders§r: each has its machine, depending on its nature.
• §6industrial crucible§r (molten metal, cast afterward): bronze, brass, solder, electrum, babbitt; duralumin under argon; zircaloy at MKII;
• §6induction furnace§r (direct alloy, ingot → ingot; steam-driven from tier 1): bronze, brass, solder, red alloy, redsteel, then invar, constantan, netherite, stainless steel from ferroalloys;
• §6converter§r and ladle metallurgy: vanadium steel, stainless steel in volume;
• §6industrial crucible (vacuum)§r: nichrome (MKII), titanium aluminide, nitinol, niobium-titanium, stellite (MKIII);
• §6industrial furnace (sintering)§r: tungsten carbide; §6arc furnace§r: osmiridium;
• §6chemical bath§r: galvanized steel (hot-dip zinc).
Molten metal is then cast in the casting machine or the industrial casting machine (continuous casting) (page “The foundry”).

The §6basic components bench§r (MKI) is the §lhand route§r for parts: no machine or power, tools that wear (§6hammer§r, §6knife§r, §6file§r), one part per click, less efficient than machines. §lOnly the bench metals§r are worked here: iron, copper, bronze, tin, brass, zinc, lead, gold, solder and steel; every other metal, in machines only. Recipes:
• §lplate§r: 2 ingots → 1; §lsheet§r: 1 plate → 1; §lfoil§r: 1 plate → 2;
• §lrod§r: 1 ingot → 1 (hammer); §lwire§r: 1 rod → 2 (hammer); §lring§r: 2 rods → 1 (hammer);
• §lspring§r: 3 wires → 1 (hammer and knife); §lgear blank§r: 5 ingots → 1 (hammer);
• §lgear§r: 1 blank + 3 plates → 1 (hammer and file); §ltube§r: 1 plate → 1 (hammer);
• §lmesh§r: 1 plate → 1 (knife and hammer); §lwire rope§r: 4 wires → 1.
It also makes the §lextrusion shapes§r (2 steel plates + the steel part that gives the profile, hammer and file), dust with the mortar and crushed ore with the hammer. Its twin machine, the §6press (forming)§r, makes the same recipes without clicking.

Every part of the §lbench metals§r (iron, copper, bronze, tin, brass, zinc, lead, gold, solder, steel) has its routes, one per station; the machine works at the §lmetal's tier§r (MKI for these metals), except the routes that carry their own tier. Bench = §6basic components bench§r:
• §lslab§r: block in the §6compactor§r; §lbillet§r: slab in the compactor; §lwire rod§r: billet in the §6rolling mill§r (→ 9); §lblock§r: 9 ingots (grid or compactor);
• §lplate§r: bench 2 ingots → 1; §6press§r 1 → 1; §6extruder§r 1 → 1; rolling mill slab → 9 or block → 9 (MKII);
• §lsheet§r: bench 1 plate → 1; §6rolling mill (calendering)§r 1 → 2; press 1 → 1;
• §lfoil§r: bench 1 plate → 2; rolling mill 1 → 3; rolling mill (calendering) 1 rod → 2;
• §lrod§r: bench 1 ingot → 1; §6wood lathe§r 1 → 2; extruder billet → 18, ingot → 2 (§l3 at MKII§r);
• §lwire§r: bench 1 rod → 2; §6wiremill§r ingot → 3, wire rod → 3, rod → 2 (MKII); extruder ingot → 4;
• §lring§r: bench 2 rods → 1; wood lathe 1 → 1; §6bender§r 1 → 2; extruder (MKII) ingot → 3;
• §lspring§r: bench 3 wires → 1; wood lathe 2 rods → 1; §6bender (coiling)§r 1 rod → 1;
• §lgear§r: bench blank + 3 plates; §6machining center (gear hobbing)§r blank + lubricant; extruder (MKIII) 5 ingots → 1; §lgear blank§r: bench 5 ingots, extruder billet → 2;
• §ltube§r: bench 1 plate → 1; bender 3 sheets → 2; extruder 1 rod → 1;
• §lmesh§r: bench 1 plate → 1; rolling mill (calendering) 3 wires → 1; §lwire rope§r: bench 4 wires → 1; wiremill 3 → 2; §6wiremill (stranding)§r 1 → 1.
§lOther metals§r are worked in machines only (die casting, continuous casting, forging). The primitive forge, clay molds and steam machines of the bronze age stay. JEI: page “Metal parts” (one page per part, all its routes).

The §6extruder§r gives the metal §lliterally the wanted shape§r: put in the metal AND an §lextrusion shape§r (a steel die whose eye has the part's profile). It goes into the §lTool slot§r and always comes back: in a machine, shaping tooling is unbreakable. For the bench metals:
• §lrod shape§r: 1 ingot → 2 rods, §l3 at MKII§r and above; 1 billet → 18 rods;
• §lwire shape§r: 1 ingot → 4 wires;
• §lring shape§r (MKII): 1 ingot → 3 rings;
• §ltube shape§r: 1 rod → 1 tube;
• §lplate shape§r: 1 ingot → 1 plate;
• §lgear shape§r (MKIII): 5 ingots → 1 gear;
• §lgear blank shape§r: 1 billet → 2 blanks.
Shapes are made at the §6basic components bench§r (or the press (forming)): 2 steel plates + the steel part that gives the profile (rod, wire, ring, gear; a ring for the tube, a gear for the blank; 3 plates for the plate shape), hammer and file. JEI shows the shape among the inputs of each recipe.

Six machines (4 tiers plus multiblock) make fasteners and basic parts:
• §6power hammer (cold heading)§r (MKII): wire rod into 8 screws, bolts or rivets at once, without offcuts; also balls and nails;
• §6bender (coiling)§r: wire into springs; precision springs (stainless steel, nitinol, tungsten) at MKIII;
• §6machining center (gear hobbing)§r: a blank (cast or forged) into a gear, with chips; lubricated helical hobbing at MKIII;
• §6wiremill (stranding)§r: wires into braided cables and wiring harnesses (MKII), ropes;
• §6power hammer§r: forged billets of metals that cannot be cast, die-forged blanks (MKII), seamless rolled rings (MKIII);
• §6machining center (grinding)§r: gems, polished stone, tungsten fasteners and precision balls (MKIII); it also reconditions worn engraving objectives.
At MKI, the lathe still makes fasteners and the wiremill makes wire, with offcuts: these machines do it better and faster.

The §6casting machine§r takes §lmolten metal§r (from the industrial crucible: remelted ingots, offcuts and sprues, or smelting reduction of ore), a §lmold§r (in the §lTool slot§r: unbreakable in a machine, it is never consumed) and a §lcooling fluid§r; it returns the part, §lsprues§r as nuggets of the same metal (to remelt: nothing is lost, the material is tied up) and the hot fluid. Molds, by tier:
• permanent §ldies§r (ingot, plate, rod, gear blank); plates and rods keep a §lriser§r of one third;
• §lsand mold§r (MKI): rough blanks of steel, copper, brass, bronze and aluminum; a mold you no longer want is shaken out in the screen (spent sand, recycled into foundry sand);
• ceramic §lshell mold§r (MKII: a wax pattern dipped in fused zirconia and fired in the industrial furnace (sintering); oil at least): bell, stainless steel and duralumin blanks;
• §lvacuum casting mold§r (MKIII, made in the industrial crucible (vacuum); liquid nitrogen at least): titanium, nichrome and stainless steel billets.
The §6molder's bench§r makes foundry sand (zircon, sand, clay), sand molds and wax patterns (beeswax from the extractor, or paraffin). Above §l1,200 °C§r, molten metal only flows through the §6logistic cable MKI§r or better (lined with fire bricks): a network that contains a wooden or bronze cable refuses it.

Each casting comes in one variant per §lfluid§r: the colder it is, the faster the part sets and the fewer sprues it leaves.
• §lair§r (no fluid, MKI): three times slower, 20% sprues;
• §lwater§r (MKI): normal time, 15% sprues; it leaves as exhaust steam (cooling tower, cryogenic unit);
• §lquench oil§r (MKII): 0.6 of the time, 10% sprues, 90% returned; §lcoolant§r (MKII) does the same and leaves hot;
• §lliquid nitrogen§r (MKIII): 0.3 of the time, 5% sprues; it comes back as gas, to liquefy again;
• §lliquid helium§r (MKIV): 0.2 of the time, 5% sprues.
Some metals need a minimum fluid: §lwater§r for steel, galvanized steel and vanadium steel; §loil§r for stainless steel, magnesium and the shell mold; §lliquid nitrogen§r for vacuum casting. The machine picks the variant of the fluid it holds; the recipe selector forces one.
The §6industrial casting machine (continuous casting)§r (MKII) turns out ingots, slabs and billets continuously, with a section die (ingot mold, plate, rod): water at MKII, oil at MKIII (stainless steel, magnesium).

The §6compactor§r (compact and multiblock) packs what is left loose:
• §lbales§r of offcuts and scrap (iron, steel, copper, aluminum): a bale melts at 95%, twice as fast as loose powder;
• concentrate §lpellets§r, with a §lbinder§r when needed (clay for chromite and ilmenite): the blast furnace and the industrial furnace (reduction) get a quarter more metal from them;
• §lgreen compacts§r of powder (steel, bronze), sintered into parts afterward;
• §lbriquettes§r and pellets of chips, sawdust and bagasse: fuels;
• packed ice and §lregolith§r bricks from other worlds (binder: quicklime), green anodes (pitch).
The binder goes in §lits own slot§r, above the “Recipe” slot. The compactor no longer makes plates or ingots.

The §6extractor§r draws out what a plant, a wood or an animal material holds, with three techniques, one per tier:
• §lhot water or steam§r (MKI): tannin and resin from wood, sugar, pectin, glue, honey and beeswax, dyes, hop extract;
• §lsolvent§r (MKII: naphtha, ethanol): plant and algae oils, cocoa butter, lanolin, essential oils; 90% of the solvent comes back: send it back to the input;
• §lsupercritical CO₂§r (MKIII): the same extracts, purer and without solvent; 90% of the CO₂ comes back too.
Each extraction leaves a §luseful residue§r: press cake (feed), pomace (compost), chips (fuel), bagasse.

The §6optical engraver§r engraves with light through an §lengraving objective§r placed in an input slot: the objective is not used up, it loses §lone point per engraving§r:
• §lglass§r objective: 64 engravings (photo-etched foils, copper mesh; MKII or better compact engraver);
• §lUV§r objective: 256 engravings (under argon: fine contact springs; MKIII or better compact engraver); it also does the glass ones;
• §lEUV§r optics: 1,024 engravings; they do them all.
The §lcleanroom§r optical engraver does every engraving, §l16 at once§r.
The tooltip shows the engravings left. Once worn, the objective becomes a §lworn objective§r: the §6machining center (grinding)§r reconditions it (new lenses or mirrors, abrasive powder) for half the cost of a new one. New objectives are built in the assembler.

A §lcomplex§r material (at least two routes, or at least three machine steps) has its “§lProcess§r” page, here and in JEI (the “Processes” category: §lR§r on the product, §lU§r on the starting material). It tells §lhow to get it§r, not the amounts:
• §lone row per route§r: the starting material on the left, then what each step makes, up to the product (framed);
• under each item, the §lprocess§r in plain words (crushing, flotation, digestion, molten salt electrolysis…); in JEI each step reads “item → [machine] → item”: the §lmachine§r sits on the arrow, drawn by its process in a frame of its tier (hover: its name; click: its recipes), its process above, the §lreagents§r going in (“+”) just above and the §lbyproducts§r coming out below; the product has a §lgold frame§r;
• at the head of each route in JEI: its number, the §lchip§r of its tier with its complexity as dots (1 simple, 2 complex, 3 very complex), its short name, then its §lstrength§r (+) and its §lweakness§r (−); the first page of a material with several routes starts with an §loverview§r (one line per route: tier, strength, number of steps); hovering the title shows the full name and the steps §lalongside§r (a reagent to prepare: anodes, cryolite; a treatment after the product: red mud, recycling);
• a long route wraps to the next line in JEI (3 steps per row, return arrow); here the middle steps are summed up and the full list is in the text.
Machines and items are clickable: the exact recipe (amounts, energy, time) is on the machine’s page. The same routes can be read on the Metallurgy chapter pages (“A metal’s routes”).

A machine that knows several recipes picks §lthe most complete one that fits§r its inputs by itself. To force one: on §lcompact MKI to MKIV machines§r (workshop machines and kit), the §6recipe square§r opens from the §lRecipes§r tab (first of the tabs on the right: it shows the locked recipe's icon), the §lRecipe§r slot or §lCtrl + R§r, and lies §lover§r the machine (Esc or ✕ closes it; it is always closed when you open the interface); on the others (multiblocks, mechanical machines, crucible furnace, coke oven), the §6Recipe§r slot (above the arrow) opens the §llist of its recipes§r:
• search is active right away: §ltype§r to filter (products and inputs); filters §lAll§r, §lReady§r (inputs present, right tier), §l★§r (favorites) and §lRecent§r (Tab: next filter);
• in the grid (mouse wheel to scroll): green line, every input is there; amber, only some of them; grayed out with a red corner, tier too high; ★ favorite, blue dot recent, padlock locked; under the grid, the hovered recipe: inputs → outputs, time at MKI → at the machine's tier, energy and required tier;
• §lclick§r or §lEnter§r (arrow keys pick): the recipe is §llocked§r and the square closes. The machine makes only that one, and its slots, tanks and ports §lrefuse everything else§r: hoppers, belts, robots and logistics put nothing else in;
• §lright-click§r or §lF§r on a recipe of the square: §lfavorite§r (kept per machine, from one game to the next); Shift + click: its recipes in JEI; an item dragged from JEI onto the grid: search by that item;
• the Delete key, the square's padlock or a §lright-click§r on the Recipe slot: the lock is removed. JEI's “+” button, or an item dragged from JEI onto the slot, also sets the lock.
An §lamber dot§r on the slot: several recipes take the inputs present, lock the right one. A §lred corner§r: the recipe needs a higher tier (“Tier too low: MKIII required”; nothing is used up).
The lock stays in the machine when it is §lbroken and placed again§r. It can also be set remotely: §lMachines§r tab of the §6logistic terminal§r, and in PioScript: §lm.lockRecipe(id)§r, §lm.unlockRecipe()§r, §lm.lockedRecipe()§r. Every workshop machine and multiblock with recipes has it, as do the crucible furnace (alloy choice), the coke oven, the kit machines and the mechanical machines (except the sifter).

Before each machine, you assemble its §6machine hull§r at the §6assembly bench§r (wrench): the tier's §6frame§r, the tier's §6circuit§r, §l2 plates and 2 rods§r of the tier's secondary metal:
• §6MKI§r: steel frame, vacuum tube circuit, brass;
• §6MKII§r: aluminum frame, transistor circuit, electrum;
• §6MKIII§r: titanium frame, microcontroller circuit, chrome;
• §6MKIV§r: tungsten frame, photonic circuit, osmiridium.
It is made without any machine of its tier: the MKI machine hull needs neither electricity nor an MKI machine.
From MKII on, the machine hull §lcontains the previous tier's hull§r (an MKI hull is upgraded into an MKII one, and so on), and the MKII to MKIV §6industrial casing§r needs §lits tier's circuit§r: all the machines of a tier, compact and multiblock, open together, with its machine hull.

§6The tier rule§r: every industrial material, component, fluid and gas has a tier (mechanical age, bronze age, MKI to MKIV). An MKI machine is §lnever§r made with an item of a higher tier; only the §6transition materials§r are made at the tier before: steel (bronze age → MKI), aluminum (MKI → MKII), titanium (MKII → MKIII), tungsten (MKIII → MKIV). For example: vacuum tube circuit and relay are MKI, the rubber belt is MKII (at MKI, a leather belt).

A machine is built like the real one: the §6machine hull§r of its tier (frame, circuit, screws and cladding included), then 2 to 4 parts of its trade:
• a §6machining center§r: motor, drill bit, cast iron bed, bearings;
• a §6centrifuge§r: motor, bladed rotor, bearings, brake;
• a §6solid combustion generator§r: fire bricks, alternator, firebox grate, flue pipe;
• an §6industrial furnace§r: heating elements, firebricks, insulation, thermocouple;
• an §6electrolyzer§r: transformer, electrodes, measuring shunt;
• a §6photolithograph§r (cleanroom): UV source, laser, positioning stage.
Each part follows the generation of the tier: DC, induction, permanent-magnet and superconducting motors; fan, heat pipe, liquid cooling, diamond heatsink; cathode-ray tube, LCD, OLED, holographic projector.
Difficulty follows the §lrole§r, in every age: a §lsimple§r machine (furnace, crusher, press, wire mill, bender) only has easy parts of its age (die, §6press die§r and §6drill bit§r in steel; the vanadium steel ones, in the assembler, yield twice as much); at MKIII and MKIV it reuses the trade parts of the previous tier and takes a single part of its own tier, its power control (§6variable frequency drive§r, §6superconducting transformer§r): it is made right after the machine hull. A §lmedium§r machine takes one part from the middle of its age (pump of the tier, cylinder, zirconium lining of the MKII chemical reactor). A §lcomplex§r machine is made of the simpler machines of its field and of late-age parts: §6centrifuge cascade§r = 2 MKIII centrifuges + compressor unit, §6semiconductor fab§r = MKII compact photolithograph + pick-and-place, photolithographs and pick-and-places with a vacuum pump, §6programmable logic controller§r = relay cabinet + absolute encoder servos, §6computer§r = a controller, §6data center§r = 2 computers, §6AI lab§r = a computer, §6quantum computer§r = a computer + cryostat, §6factory AI core§r = AI lab + quantum computer; cooled §6spent fuel pool§r (MK3 pump and heat exchanger), §6hot cell§r (MKIII nuclear) with a sapphire window, §6tokamak§r with an MK4 vacuum pump.

Multiblock §6controllers§r take the machine hull of their tier, two core components of their trade and two parts of the core's generation. §6Casings§r and §6upgrade kits§r are made of the tier material.

A single §6plank§r per wood type; all the rest of the wood is unique and made at the §6carpenter bench§r, then in machines:
• §6lath§r (sawn plank): drying racks, hive frames, compost bins;
• §6pegs§r and §6beam§r (planks, hide glue) → §6wooden machine bed§r, the base of the cheese press;
• §6barrel stave§r (bent plank): barrels, fermenting vats, wooden pipes;
• §6treated wood§r (planks and creosote, in the autoclave): railroad ties, hulls of the first ships;
• §6veneer§r (peeled log) + resin glue → §6plywood§r: passenger car interiors; sawdust, wood chips and glue → §6particle board§r: boxcar floors.

The §6DC motor§r (MKI): a §6stator§r (electrical steel laminations, enameled wire, insulating varnish), a §6rotor§r (laminations, rod, copper coil), two §6ball bearings§r, a §6cast iron housing§r and four screws. At the assembly bench, a simpler way: a stator of steel plates and copper coils.

Then one generation per tier, each made of its own parts (motors are no longer upgraded):
• §6induction§r (MKII): roller bearings, magnesium housing, capacitors and transistors;
• §6permanent magnet§r (MKIII): rare-earth magnets, ceramic bearings, titanium housing, variable frequency drive;
• §6superconducting§r (MKIV): superconducting coils in a cryostat, tharsium magnets, magnetic bearings.
Only machines with a spinning part need a motor.

From sand to chip, at MKII:
• §6silicon§r + hydrochloric acid + hydrogen → §6polysilicon§r (chemical reactor);
• §6crystallizer (crystal pulling)§r (quartz, argon) → §6single-crystal boule§r → §6industrial saw (wafer slicing)§r (ultrapure water) → §6wafers§r;
• §6industrial furnace (diffusion)§r: phosphine → N-doped wafer, diborane → P-doped;
• §6photolithograph§r (both wafers, amethyst, photoresist) → exposed wafer → §6chemical bath (acid etching)§r (hydrofluoric acid) → §6etched wafer§r;
• the etched wafer gives §6transistors§r (assembler, gold wire), §6integrated circuits§r (pick-and-place) and §6memory chips§r (assembler) in DIP packages, and the §6microprocessor§r (semiconductor fab).
At MKIII: epitaxy (chemical reactor), implantation, then §lfine etching§r (fluorite, photomask): SMD circuits, MOSFETs, processors. At MKIV: §lEUV etching§r (tin) and photonic chips.

Stored energy follows the ages; §6electrolyte§r (sulfuric acid and water, in the mixer) goes into the first ones:
• §6zinc-carbon cell§r (MKI: zinc, graphite): small devices, like the surface scanner;
• §6lead-acid battery§r (MKI: lead plates, primitive plastic): energy buffers, battery cars, the starter of diesel locomotives, MKI rovers;
• §6NiMH cell§r (MKII: nickel, rare earths): MKII buffers, cars, rovers and drones;
• §6lithium-ion cell§r (MKIII: lithium carbonate, cobalt, graphite, copper and aluminum foils): MKIII cars and drones; §6fuel cell stack§r (platinum, membranes): MKIII rovers; §6vanadium flow cell§r (vanadium, membranes, pump): buffers and substations;
• §6solid-state cell§r (MKIV: lithium, zirconia ceramic): MKIV buffers, cars, rovers and drones.
Substations switch with §6circuit breakers§r, then §6solid-state relays§r at MKIII.

One circuit per generation, made of the components of its age:
• §6vacuum tube circuit§r (MKI): vacuum tubes, carbon resistors, paper capacitors, a relay and connectors, on a §6perforated board§r (advanced components bench, 1) or an §6etched board§r with solder (assembler, 2). Its parts are made at the §6redstoner bench§r, each with its Bronze Age part (relay with §6red alloy wire§r, resistors with a §6coal ball§r; perforated board at the steam press): it opens MKI without any MKI machine but through the steam machines; in the assembler, with the etched board, solder and plastic;
• §6transistor circuit§r (MKII, pick-and-place): printed circuit board, transistors and diodes (discrete logic), film resistors, capacitors, quartz;
• §6microcontroller circuit§r (MKIII): multilayer board, SMD circuits, MOSFETs, niobium capacitors, planar inductor, gold connectors;
• §6photonic circuit§r (MKIV): ceramic substrate, photonic chip, BGA packages, supercapacitors.
The board §levolves§r: each circuit contains §l2 circuits of the previous generation§r (for 2 circuits); at MKII, the vacuum tube circuits replace the brass connectors.
Computing follows the same generations: tube computers and core memory (Gen 1), microprocessor, DRAM and hard disk (Gen 2), multicore processor and SSD (Gen 3), neuromorphic processor, HBM memory and NVMe SSD (Gen 4).

Motion and fluid parts come in §l4 generations§r, each made of the material of its tier:
• §6bearings§r: steel ball, roller, ceramic, magnetic;
• §6pistons§r: pneumatic, hydraulic, high-pressure, nitinol actuator;
• §6planetary gearsets§r, §6bladed rotors§r, §6engine blocks§r, §6centrifugal pumps§r (cast iron, aluminum, titanium, then magnetic pump), §6heat exchangers§r, §6valves§r, §6transformers§r, §6generators§r, §6servomotors§r.
Each generation is made in the assembler (MKI parts also at the assembly or metalworker bench), and most of them are §llineages§r: the version of a tier §lcontains§r the one of the previous tier, which is upgraded (one for one), and does not pay again for what it already brings:
• §6motors§r: the induction motor takes the DC motor (its stator and rotor), the permanent magnet motor takes the induction motor, the superconducting motor takes the permanent magnet one (its housing);
• §6bearings§r (ball → roller → ceramic → magnetic), §6pistons§r (pneumatic → hydraulic → high-pressure → nitinol actuator), §6planetary gearsets§r, §6bladed rotors§r, §6centrifugal pumps§r (impeller and volute come from the previous pump), §6heat exchangers§r (the previous exchanger serves as a preheater);
• §6copper coil§r → §6enameled coil§r (MKII, rewound with enameled wire and impregnated with epoxy) → §6superconducting coil§r (MKIII: Nb-Ti cable on an enameled coil, epoxy and §lliquid helium§r, in the MKIII assembler); §6oil-filled transformer§r → §6dry-type transformer§r → §6superconducting transformer§r; the §6inductive sensor§r takes an enameled coil.
Machines take them according to their trade (see “Machines by roles”).

A factory's walls are built from basic shapes, with the §lsame template§r at every tier, at the §6assembly bench§r:
• §6frame§r: 2 plates, 8 rods, 4 screws, 2 bolts, 4 springs, 2 rings → 2; §6sheet metal block§r: the same recipe with 8 sheets instead of the rods → 2;
• §6casing§r: a metal block, 4 beams, 8 tier parts, 8 sheets, 6 plates and a frame → 6 (8 in the §6assembler (arc welding)§r, under argon from MKII);
• §lglass casing§r (industrial glass): a casing and 4 technical glasses (tempered, borosilicate, fused silica, sapphire by tier) → 1;
• §lspecialized casings§r (robotics hub, absorber, Stabilizer, Simulator): a casing of the tier and the machine's signature material → 4; their glass: a specialized casing and 4 sapphire glasses → 1.
The metals: §lMKI§r steel (bronze springs, copper rings; casing: bronze block, brass rods, copper plates); §lMKII§r aluminum and duralumin (casing: galvanized steel beams, electrum rings); §lMKIII§r titanium (nickel springs; casing: stainless steel block and plates, vanadium steel springs); §lMKIV§r tungsten (gold rings; casing: titanium beams, tungsten gears, osmium plates).
An MKI casing takes about 5 ingots of metal, and each tier costs roughly twice as much, in rarer metals. A §ltier N casing is made with tier N−1 machines§r: it is what builds the first multiblocks of tier N.

Wall blocks required by the structures (1.0.4). Fireboxes, coils and stackable blocks give, like a casing, their §ltier§r to the multiblock (the weakest wins); when the machine works, the fireboxes light up. The cleanroom has no tier: its machines have a fixed capacity.
• §6firebox walls§r MKI to MKIV: the bed of furnaces (industrial furnace, blast furnace, pyrolysis oven, coke oven battery, converter, dryer, industrial crucible, boiler and solid combustion generator); a large barred grate in front of the embers (lining of the tier: fireclay, alumina, zirconia, ceramic composite), neighboring fireboxes form a single grate;
• §6heating coils§r MKI to MKIV: the shaft of the industrial blast furnace and the pyrolysis oven; resistance wire on ceramic combs — annealed §liron§r, §lplatinum§r, §lnichrome§r, §ltungsten§r; always vertical, neighboring coils form a single winding;
• §6cleanroom§r: §6casing§r, §6glass§r and §6ceiling filter§r of the optical engraver, the semiconductor fab, the data center and the quantum computer (a port can replace a casing);
• §lstackable blocks§r MKI to MKIV, always vertical: the §6drill string§r in the middle of the drill, the §6column pump§r in the middle of the fluid pump and the pumpjack; the §6turbine rotor§r as a column of 7 in the middle of the §lsteam and gas turbines§r (required: it spins while running). Stacked, identical blocks form a single column.
All are made at the §6assembly bench§r (wrench), or in the §6Assembler§r.

Two simple multiblocks, built to Guillaume's structure (blueprint: Shift + right-click the controller).
• §6industrial chimney§r: a sheet-metal tower on a casing base, the simple version of the §6cooling tower§r. It §lvents to the air§r turbine exhaust steam and surplus steam (gas port), with no energy (natural draft) and §lreturns nothing§r: half as fast as the tower, which gives the water back;
• §6item silo§r: stores §la single item§r in bulk (same item, same NBT), in very large amounts: §l92,160§r items at MKI, ×4 per tier. Item port as input to fill, as output to empty; the controller screen shows the contents, a comparator the level. When broken, the controller §lkeeps its stock§r: place it again to get it back.

Machine heat always ends up in a §6cooling tower§r (or a §6cryogenic unit§r):
• §lwater-cooled§r: the machine gives §lsteam§r; the §6steam turbine§r draws power from it (80 FE per mB) and rejects as much §lexhaust steam§r, a gas no machine takes: the tower condenses it into water, to send back to the machines;
• §lcoolant-cooled§r: the hot coolant goes through the §6industrial boiler§r with water: steam for the turbine, and the coolant comes back §lcold§r; or straight through the tower (no power);
• surplus steam: the tower condenses it too;
• §lsmall setup§r (a bronze age boiler): the steam drives the §6steam engine§r and its §6dynamo§r, with no exhaust steam to condense. A steam turbine needs the tower (table in the annex).
The tower is free but §l5% of the water leaves with the plume§r: plan a make-up (pump, noria). The §6cryogenic unit§r condenses without loss, at the cost of power.
The tower is §lbuilt§r: round casing basin (controller and ports on the rim), §6frame§r legs letting the air in between them, packing row (empty: built in) above, then a thin §6concrete shell panel§r shell, hollow and open at the top. Running, rain falls from the packing and the plume rises high above the throat.

One cable for everything: §litems, fluids, gases (steam included), energy, heat, pressure and data§r. Place it to link your machines: wherever it touches a block holding a resource it can carry, a §lplug§r appears.
• §lRight-click§r any cable (empty hand): the network §6diagram§r, a simplified schematic (a 500-block cable = one line) where everything is set;
• as long as you have set nothing, the network is in §lauto mode§r: whatever a machine ejects into its plug goes to the other connected machines;
• up to §l16 channels§r per network, at every level: each one carries a type, with its own filter and distribution;
• the §6wrench§r cuts or restores the aimed side; Shift + wrench: the diagram.
• §6cover frame§r: right-click a cable with it, it fits around it; then right-click the frame with a §lfull block§r (stone, planks…): the cable hides inside and keeps working. The §6file§r removes the cover, then the frame; the wrench works as on the bare cable, and while you hold it, hidden cables show through; breaking returns the cover, then the frame.
Six levels (wood, bronze, MKI to MKIV) that all connect: the network runs at the level of its weakest cable (the “weak link”).

A §lchannel§r = a type + a filter + points (the machines taking part). Several channels of the same type are allowed: three item channels with different filters, for example.
• §ltype§r: only those the level allows (a refused type is grayed out, with the reason);
• §lfilter§r: allow or deny list, 9 slots: item, §l#tag§r (#forge:ingots/iron), §l@mod§r (@pioneers), fluid or gas; empty = everything passes;
• §lcondition§r: always, or only while a cable of the network receives (or does not receive) redstone;
• §lmax flow§r: limits the channel below the level's rate (each channel has its own rate);
• the §lorder§r matters: the first channel of the right type gets what a machine ejects into its plug.
More than 16 channels (two networks joining): the most recent stay active, the others wait, grayed out; nothing is lost.

Each channel shares what it carries between its outputs:
• §lRound robin§r (default): outputs are served one after another;
• §lPriority first§r: the highest-priority output is filled before the next;
• §lProportional§r: shared according to each output's weight (1 to 16);
• §lBalanced§r: the emptiest output first (a buffer set to “Both” then makes a good tank).
Sources are always emptied by decreasing priority. Nothing ever goes back to the machine that just sent it. The rate applies §lper channel§r: two item channels on an MKI cable each move 32 items per second.

Heat and compressed air are not carried, they are §lconducted§r: a heat (or pressure) channel gives the whole network §lone shared body§r (its capacity, its air volume: the sum of the cables'), which exchanges with every point each tick.
• A point's role is a §lvalve§r: Input = heat (air) only goes from the machine to the cable; Output = only from the cable to the machine; Both = free;
• losses to the air and leaks: those of the network's cables;
• level limits: above its temperature (bronze 1,200 °C, MKI 2,227 °C) the body accepts nothing more; no air above its pressure (3 / 5 / 10 / 20 / 40 bar, from bronze to MKIV);
• no distribution for these channels (physical exchanges).
A 500-block cable costs no more than a 5-block one: no cable-by-cable computation.

Rates per channel, from wood to MKIV:
• §6items§r: 4 / 12 / 32 / 128 / 512 / 2,048 per second;
• §6fluids§r: 160 / 400 / 1,000 / 4,000 / 16,000 / 64,000 mB/t;
• §6gases and steam§r: — / 640 / 2,000 / 8,000 / 32,000 / 128,000 mB/t;
• §6energy§r: — / — / 16,384 / 131,072 / 1,048,576 / 8,388,608 FE/t;
• §6heat§r: — / 32 / 64 / 256 / 1,024 / 4,096 J/t/K; §6pressure§r: — / 20 / 40 / 120 / 360 / 1,080 L/t/bar;
• §6data§r: MKI and better, 2,000 / 16,000 / 128,000 / 1,024,000 MB/t.
Limits (never a leak or an explosion, a plain refusal): wood up to 400 K; bronze up to its own melting point (1,200 °C: molten tin, copper, brass); MKI §llined with fire bricks§r: every molten metal; corrosive and cryogenic fluids from MKII on (§lPTFE lining§r). Recipes: wood at the carpenter (planks, iron ring), bronze at the coppersmith (plates, ring, solder), MKI on the grid (steel tubes, copper wire, fire brick, glass pane), then MKII (PTFE, stainless tube), MKIII (titanium tube), MKIV (space). Transparent: 4 cables + the tier's glass.
Every MKI-or-better network is also the §6digital network§r: supervision, shared computing and data banks, with no channel to create.

The §6superconducting cable§r carries §lenergy§r only, lossless, up to §l1,073,741,824 FE/t§r per channel (128 times MKIV): to link the big power plants. It only connects to itself and to machines (never to the other cables), and keeps its 16 energy channels.
• MKIII route: titanium plates, NbTi cable, stainless tubes and a superconducting coil → 4;
• MKIV route, more profitable: the §6high-temperature superconducting tape§r instead of the NbTi cable → 8.

Everything connects to the same system:
• a single §6logistic cable§r for everything (items, fluids, gases, energy, heat, pressure, data), in 6 levels: wood (Mechanical Age), bronze (Bronze Age: gases and steam, heat, pressure), then MKI (steel tubes, copper core, fire-brick lining and glass core: energy, data and molten metal), MKII (PTFE lining: corrosive and cryogenic fluids), MKIII (titanium), MKIV (space). Every level connects to the others (speed of the weakest);
• water vapor is a §lgas§r like any other: the boiler dome pushes it into the cable, which delivers it to steam machines (up to 8 bar) as well as to the multiblocks' §6gas ports§r;
• §lright-click§r a cable: the network diagram (channels, who gives, who receives, through which face);
• §6buffers§r: MKI when crafted, then right-click with an §6upgrade kit§r (contents stay); in creative, §lcreative buffers§r with endless contents;
• one wrench for everything: bronze, iron or §6steel§r.

A §6buffer§r is an in-line store, by family and in four tiers:
• items: 18 / 27 / 45 / 54 slots;
• fluids: 64,000 / 256,000 / 1,024,000 / 4,096,000 mB;
• gas: 128,000 / 512,000 / 2,048,000 / 8,192,000 mB;
• energy: 16 M / 128 M / 1,024 M / 8,192 M FE;
• heat: 4,000 / 16,000 / 64,000 / 256,000 J/K (it glows as it heats up);
• compressed air: 4,000 / 16,000 / 64,000 / 256,000 L, at the tier's maximum pressure.
Broken, it keeps its contents; its front shows the level and a comparator reads it. It is crafted at MKI; an §6upgrade kit§r (right-click) moves it to the next tier with nothing lost. In creative, creative buffers hold billions of endless contents with no flow limit of their own: only the cables cap it.

Open the counter: as long as it isn't linked, §lthe grid and the bottom line say what is missing§r, and the §lInfo tab§r (the “i”) gives the §6Network§r line, the details with positions and what to do. In the order you build the line:
§l1.§r a §6small pulley§r with a §lvertical axis§r above the counter, 8 blocks at most, empty column in between;
§l2.§r a §6rope§r from that pulley to the line; the line's pulleys at the same height;
§l3.§r nothing solid under the ropes: a parcel hangs a good half block under the rope (the counter names the block in the way);
§l4.§r at the end, a small pulley §lright above the hatch§r (the block in the middle of the crate's top), not above a corner;
§l5.§r a §6formed crate§r (3 × 3 cross-section, 3, 5 or 7 long; Shift + right-click the hatch: the blueprint);
§l6.§r rotation: without it, the counter sees the crate but says “Rope stopped”.
The counter rereads its network by itself (chunk loaded, block removed): no need to break it.

The §6caravan relay§r (carpenter: 2 logs, 4 planks, an iron ring, a bronze ring, a hay bale) is a hitching post with a water trough and a manger. Animals stop §lin front of it§r. It works with the §lcontainer touching it§r (below or on a side: chest, barrel, buffer, port of a warehouse crate…). Its screen:
• §lOrder§r: load (from the container into the packs), unload (from the packs into the container), both (unloads what the filter does not keep, loads what it keeps), pass (simple stop);
• §lFilter§r: 9 items (click with the item; right-click: clear); empty = all;
• §lLeave§r: after the wait, packs full or packs empty; the §lwait§r (10 s by default) is always a minimum;
• §lManger§r: rations (wheat, carrot, oats, barley, apple = 1; animal feed = 4; hay bale = 9; cactus = 2, camels only). An animal eats at a relay once it has walked half of its 256 blocks (512 for a camel); hungry, the caravan goes 30% slower.
Only its owner (who placed it) and their clan set it up; named at an anvil, its name becomes the stop’s name.

The §6route sheet§r (tanner: 3 paper, a leather, a bronze ring): §lclick relays§r, they are added in order (§l96 blocks at most§r between two, 16 stops). Click in the air: the route (move up, move down, remove, §lloop§r or §lback and forth§r). To form the caravan: hold the followers on leads and §lclick the lead animal§r, they get tied behind it in single file; then §lclick the lead animal with the sheet§r: it sets off to the nearest relay. Sneaking: it stops. Looking at an animal shows its stop, distance, pack and state.
On the way, the caravan §lkeeps its chunks loaded§r around the lead (even far from you), waits for a straggler and, stuck for 45 s, jumps the obstacle and tells you. If a relay of the route disappears, it stops and tells you.

Ways to make power at every stage:
• §lMechanical Age§r: water wheels, wooden-sail windmill, horse mill: §6rotation§r, turned into power by a §6dynamo§r;
• §lBronze Age§r: the §6steam engine§r on a dynamo (~1,900 FE/t; the dynamo sets its own load to draw the most from the source);
• §lIndustrial Age§r: three fuel generators, each MKI to MKIV and as a multiblock: the §6solid combustion generator§r (coal, coke, wood, pellets, biochar…), the first power after the dynamo; the §6liquid combustion generator§r (diesel, gasoline, kerosene, ethanol, oils…); the §6gas turbine§r (natural gas, methane, biogas, wood gas…); and five fuel-free §6renewable§r generators in four tiers: §6solar panel§r, §6wind turbine§r, §6dam turbine§r, §6geothermal plant§r, §6microbial fuel cell§r;
• §lbig multiblocks§r: boiler and steam turbine (after the gas turbine);
• §lthe atom§r: fission, fusion, RTG; §lspace§r: solar absorber, lightning collector.

One rule: §l1 burn tick = 1,024 FE§r, doubled in the combustion generators (×2 efficiency: one coal gives 3.3 million FE there; coke burns twice as long).
• §6dynamo§r: rotation becomes energy (§l1 FE per 1 J§r); its load adapts itself to the source to draw the most from it (a steam engine: ~1,900 FE/t in direct drive); right-click: its interface;
• three fuel generators, MKI to MKIV and as multiblocks (they give energy out through every face): the §6solid combustion generator§r (coal, coke, charcoal, wood, pellets, biochar…; §l×2 efficiency§r: 2,048 FE/t at MKI on coal; the first power after the dynamo, nothing electric at MKI), the §6liquid combustion generator§r (creosote, benzol, diesel, gasoline, kerosene, ethanol, oils, living fuels; ×2 too: 8,192 FE/t on diesel at MKI) and the §6gas turbine§r (natural gas, methane, propane, hydrogen, biogas, wood gas, coke oven gas, blast furnace gas…: 4,096 FE/t on natural gas at MKI, ×1.25 as a multiblock);
• §6industrial boiler§r: 24 mB of steam per burn tick, §l1 mB of water → 8 mB of steam§r like steam boilers (no electricity);
• §6steam turbine§r (multiblock): §l80 FE per mB§r: boiler + turbine yield almost as much per coal as the solid combustion generator (0.94 times) for far more power, and it rejects §lexhaust steam§r, to be condensed back into water (see “The water loop”).

Maximum output in FE/t (MKI / MKII / MKIII / MKIV), if the fuel keeps up:
• §6solid combustion generator§r (×2 efficiency): coal 2,048 / 8,192 / 32,768 / 131,072; §6liquid§r (×2): diesel 8,192 / 32,768 / 131,072 / 524,288; §6gas turbine§r: natural gas 4,096 / 16,384 / 65,536 / 262,144;
• §6multiblocks§r (×1.25 efficiency, ×8 per tier; combustion: ×2 like the compact one): solid combustion on coal 8 k / 65 k / 524 k / 4.2 M; liquid combustion on diesel 33 k / 262 k / 2.1 M / 16.8 M; §6gas turbine§r on methane 20 k / 164 k / 1.3 M / 10.5 M; §6steam turbine§r 51 k / 410 k / 3.3 M / 26 M;
• §6dynamo§r: 2,048 / 8,192 / 32,768 / 131,072 at most; a steam engine gives ~1,900, a windmill ~1,000, a horse mill ~190;
• §6renewables§r (MKI → MKIV, at best): solar 512 → 32,768 per panel, wind 512 → 32,768, dam 512 → 32,768, geothermal 512 → 32,768, microbial cell 256 → 16,384;
• §6reactors§r: fission Gen I ~3.5 M, Gen II ~9.4 M, Gen III ~12.5 M; §6tokamak§r ~6 M, §6heliotron§r ~10 M;
• §6solar absorber§r: 2 M per collector at the Sun (242 M at most); §6lightning collector§r 12 k to 56 k on average in a thunderstorm; RTG 4,096.
An ultimate cable carries 8.4 MFE/t.
§lA full generator doesn't stop§r (1.0.4): it stays lit and burns its fuel as usual; what no longer fits in its storage is §llost§r (the screen says “surplus lost”). Connect an energy buffer or machines so nothing goes to waste.

Still §l1 burn tick = 1,024 FE§r, but refined fuels are worth far more: gasoline 3,072 FE/t, diesel and high-octane gasoline 4,096 FE/t, LPG, kerosene, biodiesel, ethanol, methanol, fuel oil.
• §6liquid combustion generator§r (compact MKI to MKIV or multiblock, ×2 efficiency): liquid fuels;
• §6gas turbine§r (compact MKI to MKIV or multiblock ×1.25): combustible gases;
• to go further, chain them: the §6industrial boiler§r burns methane, wood gas and coke oven gas and makes steam for a §6steam turbine§r.

The simplest: placed under the sky, it gives up to §l512 / 2,048 / 8,192 / 32,768 FE/t§r (MKI to MKIV) with the sun at its highest.
• §lsun§r: output follows its height; nothing at night;
• §lweather§r: rain ×0.35, storm ×0.2;
• §lworld§r: the closer to the star, the more it gives: Mercury ×4, Mars ×0.43, belt ×0.13, Jupiter ×0.04; no atmosphere ×1.3 (Moon); Venus clouds and Titan haze ×0.1; on an §6orbital station§r, almost always in sunlight.
Touching panels §lpool their energy§r: lay them out as a field, one cable at the edge is enough. Two panels per recipe.

A wind turbine has three parts: §lthe mast§r, which you build yourself (any blocks: stone, quartz, iron…), the §6nacelle§r (a full block) placed §lon top§r, and a §6wind rotor§r placed §lon the front face§r of the nacelle (it takes its facing and turns with it). §lNo rotor, no power.§r Up to §l512 / 2,048 / 8,192 / 32,768 FE/t§r.
• §lrotor§r: the nacelle runs at the lower tier of the two (an MKIV nacelle with an MKII rotor gives MKII); the bigger the rotor, the bigger its disc;
• §laltitude§r: 10% at sea level, full 100 blocks higher;
• §lrotor disc§r (radius 3 / 4 / 5 / 6 blocks around the hub: the mast must be at least that tall): keep it clear, output follows the §lsquare§r of its clear share;
• §lwind§r: slow gusts (×0.55 to ×1.15), rain ×1.25, storm ×1.6;
• §lair§r: nothing without an atmosphere (Moon, stations); Mars ×0.375, Titan ×1.5, Nether ×0.5.
The nacelle screen tells you when the rotor is missing. Turbines placed before this version get their rotor back in front of them (or as an item if the spot is taken).

It pumps water into hot rock and runs the returning steam through a turbine. Up to §l512 / 2,048 / 8,192 / 32,768 FE/t§r.
• §ldepth§r: nothing above y 48, full at y −60: dig down;
• §lNether§r: always at its best;
• §llava or magma§r touching it: +10% per block, +30% at most;
• §lwater§r: 2 / 8 / 32 / 128 mB/t at full power, through any side (pump, logistic cable); with too little water, output follows.
It is a §lfull cube§r: lava or magma touch it on any side, so set it against a lava flow.

• §6industrial cracker (catalytic cracking)§r (zeolite): heavy gas oil → §lgasoline§r and propylene;
• §6industrial chemical reactor (catalytic reforming)§r (platinum): naphtha → reformate, benzene, toluene, xylene and §lhydrogen§r;
• §6industrial cracker (hydrocracking)§r (nickel): gas oil, fuel oil → kerosene, diesel; §lsweetens sour crude§r (H₂S → Claus process → sulfur); bitumen → synthetic crude;
• §6industrial cracker (delayed coking)§r: residues → §lpetroleum coke§r (fuel, graphite, anodes);
• §6industrial cracker (steam cracking)§r: naphtha, ethane, propane → §lethylene§r, propylene, butadiene, benzene.
The §6mixer§r makes high-octane gasoline and lubricant.

• §6industrial chemical reactor (Haber-Bosch)§r: steam reforming of natural gas (hydrogen) and §lHaber-Bosch§r; ammonia → §6nitric acid§r (Ostwald, platinum) → ammonium nitrate; urea in the autoclave → §lfertilizer§r (works like bone meal);
• §6electrolyzer (membrane electrolysis)§r (chlor-alkali): brine → chlorine, caustic soda, hydrogen; chlorine + hydrogen → hydrogen chloride, hydrochloric acid;
• §6industrial electrolyzer (fluorine)§r: fluorite + sulfuric acid → hydrogen fluoride → §lfluorine§r, hydrofluoric acid, and §luranium hexafluoride§r (toward nuclear).
In the §6chemical reactor§r: cumene, phenol, acetone, epoxy, biodiesel, nitrobenzene, aqua regia, oleum, bleach, chloroform, refrigerant, bromine.

The road to the §ltransistor circuit§r (MKII):
• silicon + hydrochloric acid + hydrogen → §6polysilicon§r (chemical reactor);
• §6crystallizer (crystal pulling)§r (quartz, argon) → boule → §6industrial saw (wafer slicing)§r (ultrapure water) → wafers;
• §6industrial furnace (diffusion)§r (phosphine or diborane) → §6photolithograph§r (amethyst, photoresist) → §6chemical bath (acid etching)§r → etched wafer → transistors, integrated circuits, memory chips;
• boards: blank epoxy board, photoresist and ferric chloride (chemical reactor) → §6printed circuit board§r;
• §6pick-and-place machine§r: printed circuit board, transistors, integrated circuit, ROM, diodes, resistors, capacitors and quartz → §ltransistor circuit§r. The §lmicroprocessor§r comes out of the §6semiconductor fab§r (multiblock).
Generation by generation, in detail: the “Components” chapter.

The first steps toward nuclear:
• §lheavy water§r: water / hydrogen sulfide isotope exchange in the §6distillation column (stripping)§r (Girdler process), then recovery; electrolyzed: §ldeuterium§r; tritiated water gives §ltritium§r;
• §llithium-6§r: lithium carbonate (lithium brine, dryer) through the centrifuge; §lboron-10§r: a byproduct of evaporation;
• carbon-13 and nitrogen-15 in the centrifuge; §lradon§r in the cracker (yellowcake);
• §luranium hexafluoride§r in the industrial electrolyzer (fluorine).

The whole library is documented §lin the game§r, in English and French:
• in the terminal, the §l?§r button (or §lF1§r) replaces the editor with the help: categories on the left, search at the top, signature, description and example of each function. §lF1§r on a word opens its help directly; a §lclick§r on a function inserts it into the program;
• in a program: §lhelp()§r lists the categories in the console, §lhelp("math")§r one category, §lhelp("fs.read")§r or §lhelp("sqrt")§r one function;
• on the examples disk, §lfonctions.txt§r holds the full reference.
Over 180 entries: the language, built-in functions, §lmath§r, string, list and dictionary methods, §ljson§r (save a state in a file), redstone, linked machines, screens, files, §lworld§r (time, weather, biome), §lcomputer§r, computing, flight computers, rail and shipping.

All of the mod's computing goes through one network: the §6logistic cable§r MKI or better (1.11: it replaces the optical fiber). Whatever it touches is on the network, at any distance. It carries three things:
• §ldata§r (in MB): a material, produced by antennas and analyzers, kept in §6data banks§r, used by labs;
• §lcomputing§r (GOPS, QOPS): §lshared power§r, like electricity: never stored, never carried, simply available to everything touching the network;
• §lsupervision§r: controllers, screens, computers and the §6network terminal§r see the connected machines.
Start by placing a §6network terminal§r against the cable: it shows everything and says what to do next.

Three ways to watch and drive your machines, from the simplest to the most powerful:
• §6relay cabinet§r and §6programmable logic controller§r: IF… THEN rules on linked machines;
• §6supervision screen§r: one linked machine's readings, big;
• §6computer§r: a PioScript program.
§lLinking a machine§r: data link tool (Shift + right-click the machine, right-click the controller), within 64 blocks, or at §lany distance§r if both touch the same cable. A computer on the cable does not even need links: the §lnetwork§r library sees every machine on the network (network.machines(), network.machine(n), network.data(), network.compute()).

Place the §6network terminal§r against any cable of the network: it uses no power. Four tabs:
• §lOverview§r: banks and stock, computing supplied and requested, current research with, for each data type, the stock against one unit's need (red = short), machines by role;
• §lData§r: the network stock type by type;
• §lMachines§r: everything touching the cable, with its role (color dot) and state;
• §lWhat next?§r: advice worked out from your network (no bank, no computing, research short of data, full banks…).

A space research project may require an §lindustrial milestone§r: an advancement of the ages earned by any player in the world.
Examples: fission (inner system), cryogenic turbopump (T3 blueprint), data center (quantum labs), heliotron (fusion drives), foundation model (singularity theory), factory core (solar simulation).
Research labs also need §lcomputing§r from the network they touch (logistic cables MKI or better, or placed against a data center):
• Mercury-Venus to Jupiter eras: §l128 GOPS§r;
• Saturn to Neptune: §l1,024 GOPS§r;
• Pluto and beyond: §l4,096 GOPS + 1 QOPS§r;
• nearby stars: §l16,384 GOPS + 8 QOPS§r.
Without it, the lab shows "Not enough computing". Extra computing still speeds research up (up to x4).

Space research opens once the world has cleared §lthree milestones§r of the ages:
• §6titanium§r (Industrial Age);
• a first §6propellant§r: the propellant plant makes §lkerolox§r (chemistry);
• a §6programmed flight computer§r: the computing of the Atomic Age.
The launch pad, rocket integration hall, module assembly table and antenna need titanium and MKII circuits; the §6research lab§r needs a §lcomputer§r.
Milestones are shared across the world: one player's advancement is enough. They show in the research tree (K key), under "Industrial milestones".

Rocket parts are built at the §6assembly bench§r (wrench). Each tier takes the §lmetal of the world§r reached by the previous one, and the components of the ages:
• §lT1-T2§r (petrochemistry): titanium, §6turbopump§r, MKII circuit, kerolox;
• §lT3-T7§r (nuclear): §6cryogenic turbopump§r, §6carbon composite panel§r, MKIII circuit, hydrolox; lunar alloy from T6;
• §lT8-T10§r: §6nuclear thermal rocket core§r (fuel assemblies, Callisto valhallite shielding), MKIV circuit;
• §lT11-T13§r: §6fusion drive§r (sulcite tape, gyrotrons), §6quantum avionics§r.
Every nose cone carries §6heat shield tiles§r and a programmed flight computer.

Each tier opens new worlds:
• T1: Earth, Moon;  T2: Mars, Phobos, Deimos;  T3: Venus;  T4: Mercury;
• T5: asteroid belt;  T6: Jupiter's moons;  T7: Saturn's;  T8: Uranus';  T9: Neptune's;  T10: Pluto and Charon;
• T11: gas giants and stations around the Sun;  T12: Alpha Centauri;  T13: TRAPPIST-1.

You need the tier of both the departure and the arrival world. Rockets of T2 and above need a §lblueprint§r, unlocked by research. Parts (plates, tanks, boosters, fins, nose cone) are crafted separately for each tier.

Vertical speed at contact decides everything:
• §6below 5 m/s§r: perfect landing;
• §6below 10 m/s§r: good landing;
• §6below 17 m/s§r: hard landing, you get hurt;
• above: §lcrash§r, explosion and heavy injuries.
Water absorbs half of the shock. An alarm warns you when you are coming down too fast.

§6Tips§r: open the parachute high, keep fuel for the last 30 blocks, brake with short bursts. Without an atmosphere (Moon, asteroids, Mercury...), only retro-rockets save you.

The §6autopilot§r lands by itself, safely; in a gas giant it guides you to an aerostat. After landing, the folded rocket is inside the capsule, as before.

The small space machines come in §lfour tiers§r, MKI to MKIV (bands and caps in the tier color):
• §6oxygen diffuser§r: 1,024 / 2,048 / 4,096 / 8,192 air blocks, flow ×2 per tier;
• §6space chunk loader§r: radius 1 to 4 chunks;
• §6force field generator§r: max radius 16 / 24 / 36 / 48 blocks;
• §6lightning collector§r: useful mast of 8 / 12 / 18 / 24 conductors, output 250 k / 500 k / 1 M / 2 M FE/t;
§lUpgrade§r at the §6assembly bench§r: the previous-tier machine + the tier's §6upgrade kit§r + 2 circuits. The big installations (greenhouse, rover garage, drone bay) are §lbuilt bases§r: their tier is that of the weakest part.

Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune are reached with a §6T11 rocket§r, after the "Atmospheric entry" research. No ground: the capsule lands on an §6aerostat§r, a floating platform at altitude 300. If there is none within 64 blocks, one deploys under you.

A full §6atmospheric suit§r (tier 4) is mandatory: without it, you fall into the abyss.

§6Layers§r (altimeter on the right of the screen):
• upper haze: 268 to 345;
• cloud deck: 170 to 265;
• storm layer: 75 to 165, frequent lightning;
• below 60: crushing pressure, even in a suit; below 25: lethal.

§6Gas pockets§r drift around players (about 14 at a time, 10 minutes each). What they hold depends on the layer and the planet:
• §lhaze§r: mostly hydrogen; helium-3 (Saturn), methane (Uranus), ammonia (Neptune);
• §lcloud deck§r: hydrogen and ammonia (Jupiter), helium-3 (Saturn), methane (Uranus), ammonia and methane (Neptune);
• §lstorms§r: §6metallic hydrogen§r (Jupiter, Saturn) or §6rain diamonds§r (Uranus, Neptune), plus gases.
One pocket in 20 is §lrich§r (triple), one in 7 in the storms.

The §6gas scanner§r: right-click in the air to reveal pockets within 96 blocks for 10 s and get a summary. Right-click a pocket to §lmark§r it for the drones.

The simulator has §lone slot per planet§r, in orbital order. While the sun shines, each installed planet yields its resources every cycle, without wearing out:
• Mercury: mercurium, iron, gold;  Venus: venusium, sulfur, basalt;
• Earth: iron, coal, diamond, emerald;  Mars: marsium, tharsium, red sand;
• Jupiter: metals of its moons;  Saturn: tigrite, krakenite, ice;
• Uranus: veronite, messinite, blue ice;  Neptune: ruachite, diamonds, blue ice.

Ports in output mode push production into the neighboring inventory. Recipes: §bpioneers:solar_simulation§r.

A §6logistic marker§r goes on §lany face§r of a chest or machine; its settings open right away. §6Logistic chests§r (9, 18, 27, 36 or 54 slots: the small ones for a provider or requester that needs no more) have one built in. Roles:
• §lred — provider§r: units may take from it;
• §lpurple — unload§r: units actively empty it;
• §lyellow — storage§r: receives harvests and surplus;
• §lblue — requester§r: kept stocked at the wanted level;
• §lgreen — buffer§r: requests, and gives back to the network.
You also set the §laccess face§r (marker on top, items through the back), the §lpriority§r, up to 9 resources — §litems (or tags: Tags tab — the icon cycles through the tag's items, the tooltip lists them), fluids, gases, energy or data§r, each with §lits own face§r (redstone through the back, iron through the top) —, §lmachine mode§r (keep a machine input full; it remembers the last ingredient received, even when the machine works faster than deliveries) and a §lcondition§r (“only if the network has less than 1,000 ingots”).

The network works §lon its own§r, twice a second, from the points you place:
• §lrequester§r (blue): it only asks; the best-suited free unit fetches what is missing — from §lstorage§r first, then §lbuffer surplus§r, and §lproviders§r only last;
• §lunload§r (purple): emptied as fast as possible, to a requester that lacks it, otherwise to storage;
• §lstorage§r (yellow): the long-term warehouse, receiving everything units bring back;
• §lbuffer§r (green): keeps its minimum level, receives the cargo of units working nearby, and the network gradually empties the rest into storage;
• §lprovider§r (red): the reserve you fill yourself (hand, pipes, hoppers); never filled by the network, used last.
The §6logistic terminal§r orders any item from the network: units deliver it into its reserve. §lWho delivers?§r On the map, §lUnits§r tab: the §lstewards§r, how many units of each kind (drones, robots, rovers, turtles) may make these deliveries at once (0: none). Lowering the limit lets units outside finish their delivery, then go home. Stewards also §lrepair damage§r: blocks destroyed by an explosion (creeper, meteor) within coverage are put back, with materials from the network. Nothing moves? An §lalert§r says so on the map, and every order shows why it is waiting.

The §lO§r key opens the §6logistic map§r, the command post: the terrain seen from above, network coverage, hubs, relays, points, live units with their path, areas, transports and alerts. Drag to move (even from an area), wheel to zoom, layers to toggle and §lFilters§r in the bottom bar to hide a type of area, point or unit. The order list is compact (▸: details), with a search and ● to hide an order from the map.
Tools:
• §lSelect§r: click a point to set it up §lremotely§r, a hub to recall it, a unit to see its battery and cargo;
• §lArea§r: the free view opens on you, to select a work area;
• §lWorks§r: the free view of the works planner;
• §lDedicated line§r: click the source (or empty ground for “the network”), then the target.
Network coverage shows as §la single surface§r. Tabs: orders, §lnetwork stock§r (with search), units and §lalerts§r (resource not found, storage full, out of energy, unreachable place, pending delivery) — a click centers the map on it, ✕ dismisses it. Alerts §lclear by themselves§r once the problem is solved; an alert that comes back soon after is marked §lrecurring§r (↻, ringed in orange on the map): the problem is structural.

A §6dedicated line§r reserves units for a shuttle from one face to another — for what markers don't do (machine → machine, fluid from one tank to another, an exact amount). For a plain provider → requester, there is no need: the network delivers on its own.
• §lone-off§r (an amount, then the order disappears) or §lrecurring§r (continuous, or keeping the target at a level);
• items, fluids, gases, §lenergy§r or data, specific or the whole family;
• source: a block, or the §lnetwork§r (it finds the resource itself);
• §lpriority§r and a §lcondition§r on network stock;
• assignment: §lAuto§r (the best candidate), a kind, or a specific hub; and the maximum number of units.
§lShift + O§r while aiming at a block creates a transport to its face. Units group deliveries from one place into a single trip, chain missions without going home, and count what is on the way: nothing is delivered twice.

The tasks of an area:
• §lFarming§r: harvests (crops, pumpkins, melons, berries, nether wart, cocoa, and column plants: sugar cane, cactus, bamboo, kelp — the base stays and regrows —, glow berries), §ltills§r the damaged blocks of the field (dirt, grass, path) and replants; settings: §lReplant§r on / off and the §lseed§r wanted (otherwise what grew there), taken from the network;
• §lLumberjack§r: fells the whole tree (leaves included, for the saplings) down to the base, and replants — the same species, or the §lchosen sapling§r; Replant can be turned off too;
• §lFishing§r (the catch goes to the network in batches of about fifteen), §lBreeding§r (always fed; beyond the set herd size, surplus adults are put down), §lBeekeeping§r (bees fed with flowers up to 3 per hive, full hives harvested as combs or bottles in the chosen share, without ever angering the bees), §lHunting§r (by laser, from 8 to 18 blocks — never in melee —, strong enough for witches; animals of breeding and beekeeping areas are spared; no creeper blasts; targets of your choice: monsters, surplus animals, or both; herd size set to 0: every animal).
§lTurtles§r have a §lrest mode§r (on by default for each area): between jobs, they sleep on the area instead of going home, using no energy, and wake up as soon as there is work — perfect for crops and breeding.
Nothing drops on the ground: loot goes into the cargo bay; when it is full, the unit empties it at the nearest §lstorage§r of the network (otherwise the hub's buffer), then resumes. With the planner or a marker in hand, areas, transports and access faces are shown in the world. Protection mods are respected.

§6Robotic upgrades§r go in a hub (for all of its units) or a relay. §lEach one has a drawback§r:
• §lspeed§r +20% — drain +25%;
• §lcargo§r +40% — speed −8%;
• §lbattery§r +40% — speed −5%;
• §lefficiency§r drain −15% — work −8%;
• §lsilk touch§r and §lfortune§r (unit tools) — slower and hungrier work;
• relay: §lrange§r +8 blocks — upkeep +50%; §lcharging§r +60% — losses +10%.
Values per card, stacking up to 4 (silk touch: 1; fortune: 3). Each card is built at the assembly bench with its own component (motors for speed, and so on).

Units compute a §lreal path§r, recomputed as soon as the goal moves or the world changes:
• on the ground, block by block: steps, drops, §lgates and doors opened then closed§r, §lwater avoided§r (swum across at the surface if needed, never along the bottom; a unit stuck underwater leaves on its thrusters), fences and lava avoided; §lroads are preferred§r;
• in the air: a straight line if clear, else over the terrain, else a 3D path (under a roof, in a cave);
• they stop §lwithin reach§r (4 blocks for mining and works, by beam), never on the block they target;
• as a last resort, §lall of them§r take off on their thrusters; a place unreachable on the ground is reported and handed to drones.
§lShift + right-click§r on a unit opens its §lsheet§r: its name (kept by the item in the hub), battery, current job, cargo, and a button to §lrecall§r it.

Every farm product follows the progression: by hand at the §lstations§r, at the §6millstone§r and mortar with rotation, then in bulk in industry:
• §6extractor (oil pressing)§r: apple, pear and orange juice, grape must, cane juice; rapeseed, sunflower, olive oil… and §lfish oil§r from every fish of the mod (plus fish meal);
• §6fermenter§r: juices and fruit → §lethanol§r and CO₂;
• §6fermenter (anaerobic digestion)§r: vegetables and fruit → §lbiogas§r, digestate and fertilizer;
• fish oil → biodiesel and glycerol, soap, lubricant, and in the §6industrial cracker (steam cracking)§r → propane, natural gas, hydrogen;
• §6hydroponic farm§r (every crop), §6tree plantation§r (wood and fruit trees), §6aquaculture farm§r (fish).

Nine crops pack into §lone block§r at the §6agricultural bench§r (no tool), like wheat into a §lhay bale§r; the block unpacks into §l9§r in the crafting grid.
Three kinds, depending on the produce:
• oak §6crate§r with handles and a branded mark: carrots, potatoes, beetroots, parsnips, radishes, sweet potatoes, onions, garlic, leeks, cabbages, cauliflowers, broccoli, artichokes, corn, apples;
• two stacked poplar §6crates§r with their label: tomatoes, bell peppers, hot peppers, eggplants, zucchini, cucumbers, strawberries, blueberries, cranberries, grapes;
• open burlap §6sack§r, stenciled with a colored band: barley, oats, rye, paddy rice, soybeans, sunflower seeds, hops.
They face §ltoward you§r when placed and compost like a hay bale. Axe for crates, hoe for sacks.

Harvested §6cotton§r is §lspun§r: §6cotton thread§r on the §ltanner's bench wheel§r (1 cotton → 2 threads, no tool), then in the §lwire mill§r (1 cotton → 3 threads).
The thread works wherever a fiber is expected:
• §6canvas§r: 3 threads at the tanner;
• §6fiber rope§r: 4 threads → 2 ropes at the tanner (knife);
• §6string§r: 2 threads in the grid;
• tool §lbindings§r (lashed handles) also accept cotton thread.

In the Industrial Age, §levery§r kitchen recipe can also be made in the electric machines, with the same ingredients:
• §6food processor§r (compact MKI to MKIV and industrial multiblock): cutting board, cooking pot, frying pan, oven, mixing bowl, churn, jar, brew kettle, smokehouse;
• §6extractor (oil pressing)§r: fruit press; §6press§r: cheese press; §6centrifuge§r: milk into cream (separator); the honey extractor (three products) goes to the food processor;
• §6dryer§r: drying rack (drying over a fire goes to the food processor, which smokes); §6fermenter§r: fermenting vat and aging barrel; §6distillation column (stripping)§r: still.
Times: 8 ticks per stroke; half the station time to cook, fry, bake, brew and distill, a quarter to dry and smoke, a fifth for preserves and fermentation, a tenth for aging. Speed doubles with each tier and the machines work in §lparallel§r. Jams and jars keep their source name (one recipe per crop).

Big dishes need §lintermediate products§r, made in advance:
• §lcutting board§r: §6tomato slices§r, §6breadcrumbs§r, §6grated cheese§r (click; sneaking, the board still gives the seeds);
• §lmixing bowl§r: §6mayonnaise§r (egg, oil, lemon or vinegar, salt), §6pesto§r, §6vinaigrette§r, §6salsa§r, §6curry paste§r, §6garlic butter§r, §6tapenade§r, §6whipped cream§r, §6masa dough§r;
• §lcooking pot§r: §6tomato sauce§r, §6ketchup§r, §6béchamel§r, §6custard§r, §6caramel§r, §6coulis§r, vegetable or chicken §6broth§r, §6fish stock§r, §6mashed potatoes§r;
• §lfrying pan§r: §6tortillas§r, §6croutons§r; §lfermenting vat§r: §6soy sauce§r, §6sauerkraut§r, §6hot sauce§r, §6kimchi§r.

Every vegetable, fruit, fish and crustacean is used in at least §lfive dishes§r. Many recipes accept a whole §lfamily§r:
• §6white sea fish§r (cod, pollock, halibut, sea bass, sole, turbot, monkfish, grouper…): brandade, fish pie, papillote, meunière, ceviche, tacos;
• §6oily fish§r (salmon, herring, mackerel, sardine, anchovy, tuna, swordfish…): escabeche, poké bowl, pissaladière, sardines in tomato sauce, rollmops, seared steak, maki, niçoise;
• §6freshwater fish§r (trout, pike, carp, zander, catfish, eel…): matelote, fish fry, fish cakes, stuffed fish, quenelles, beer-braised fish;
• §6reef fish§r (clownfish, pufferfish, lionfish, piranha, barracuda…): island curry, tropical ceviche, spicy soup, creole fritters;
• §lany fish§r: baked whole fish, skewers, fisherman's stew, curry, stock;
• §6crustaceans§r: bisque, pasta, risotto, seafood paella, gumbo, platter; and dishes of their own (lobster thermidor, crab cakes, shrimp cocktail, crayfish étouffée…).

A few ideas, station by station (JEI shows everything):
• §lmixing bowl§r: Caesar salad, niçoise, coleslaw, gazpacho, tzatziki, maki, smoothies, lassi, sandwiches (BLT, deluxe burger, lobster roll);
• §lcooking pot§r: soups (barley, corn, cauliflower, parsnip, pea, minestrone, caldo verde, ramen, miso), curries (aloo gobi, saag, pumpkin, sweet potato), risottos, pot-au-feu, choucroute garnie, stuffed cabbage, apricot tagine, orange chicken, sweet and sour pork;
• §loven§r (raw preparations made in the mixing bowl): moussaka, gratins, parmigiana, tian, tarts (fruit, lemon, Tatin, pear, plum, apricot, strawberry), clafoutis, Black Forest, cheesecake, breads (multigrain, rye, seed, corn, banana, zucchini), muffins, scones, pizzas;
• §lfrying pan§r: popcorn, parsnip and sweet potato fries, gyoza, samosas, fritters, creole fritters, tempura, croquettes.

Ten baker's dishes, all made the same way: the §lraw dough§r in the §6mixing bowl§r, then the §6bread oven§r. They can be placed on the table like other dishes.
• §6baguette§r (bread dough + flour, 2 per batch) and §6pretzel§r (dough, salt, butter; quick to eat);
• §6brioche§r (flour, butter, eggs, sugar, yeast, milk) and §6pound cake§r (flour, butter, eggs, sugar in equal parts): very filling, keeps you fed longer;
• §lpuff pastry§r viennoiseries: §6chocolate croissant§r (speed), §6raisin swirl§r (custard, raisins; haste), §6kouign-amann§r (butter and sugar: absorption), §6apple turnover§r (regeneration);
• small cakes, four per batch: §6madeleines§r (lemon; speed) and §6brownies§r (chocolate, cocoa; strength).
Kitchen robots and the food processor make them too.

A sandwich is put together in the §6mixing bowl§r: §lbread§r and toppings from the kitchen; each batch makes §l2 sandwiches§r. They can be placed on the table like other dishes.
• §6cheese sandwich§r (cheese, butter) and §6pâté sandwich§r (game pâté, butter);
• §6tuna sandwich§r (grilled tuna steak, tomato, mayo; water breathing) and §6club sandwich§r (roast chicken, bacon, lettuce, tomato, mayo: the most filling, a little strength);
• §6egg mayo sandwich§r, §6chicken sandwich§r, §6smoked salmon sandwich§r (cream, cucumber);
• §6veggie sandwich§r (lettuce, tomato, cucumber; regeneration);
• §6panini§r: baguette, tomato, cheese and pesto in the mixing bowl, then §ltoasted in the frying pan§r.
Kitchen robots and the food processor make them too.

Sweets are cooked in the §6cooking pot§r: §lsugar§r (cane or beet) and a flavor. Small and quick to eat, they are not very filling but give a §lshort, gentle effect§r (10 to 20 seconds).
• §6barley sugar§r (lemon; speed) and §6lollipop§r (orange juice, stick; speed);
• §6butter caramels§r (caramel, butter, cream; regeneration) and §6chocolate truffles§r (chocolate, cream, cocoa; strength);
• §6fruit jellies§r (jam fruits, lemon; haste);
• §6marshmallows§r (sugar, egg; jump boost) and §6nougat§r (honey, egg, raisins; absorption);
• §6sour drops§r (lemon, orange; night vision).
One pot makes 4 to 8 sweets. Kitchen robots and the food processor make them too.

The §6aquarium§r (potter, with a file: 4 panes, 2 iron rods, 1 resin glue → 2) is a thick glass block in a steel frame; placed side by side, aquariums §ljoin§r into a single tank (up to 512 blocks).
• §lwater bucket§r: fills the lowest empty cell of the tank;
• §lfish bucket§r (the mod's fish caught alive, cod, salmon, pufferfish, tropical fish) or a §lraw fish in hand§r (right-click, one fish used, its size kept): it joins the tank if there is room: §l16 spaces per water cell§r, a fish takes one space per 12.5 cm of its real size (tropical fish 1, perch 2, cod or salmon 6, tuna 18: a big fish needs a big tank); the tank must contain water;
• §lcrustacean bucket§r: right-click a crustacean (crab, crayfish…) with a water bucket; §l2 per cell§r, they walk on the bottom;
• §lempty bucket§r: takes back a dweller, otherwise water.
Fish swim in smooth loops at a steady pace, their tails beating in time with their swimming, and pass over the decor; no hunger, no spawning: it is living decoration. A caught §6gray reef shark§r (14 spaces) needs a tank of at least §l18 water cells§r. Breaking an aquarium releases its dwellers and returns the items of its decor.

A new aquarium holds §lonly water§r. §lShift + right-click§r (sneaking, whatever you hold) opens the §6bottom layout§r: the tank seen from above, north at the top, with its status (dwellers, spaces, water cells).
• each §lbottom§r cell offers §l4 spots§r: the bigger the tank, the more you can lay out;
• §lFloor§r: sand, red sand, §6black sand§r, gravel, dirt, clay, pebbles (a 2 px layer);
• §lPlants§r: seagrass, §6kelp§r (over half the water height), tube, brain, bubble, fire and horn corals (alive), glowing sea pickles — a plant takes root in a floor; the §6lily pad§r floats on the surface;
• §lDecor§r: seashells, rock, mossy rock, driftwood, amphora, sunken chest (it lets out bubbles).
Pick the element in the palette, then §lclick§r a spot: one item is taken from your inventory (the previous one is returned). §lRight-click§r: remove the element, then the floor; the item comes back. Fish swim over the decor.

To stop filling your inventory with seeds and fish that do not stack (each one has its genes or its size), three tanner's bags. Each §lslot§r holds §lone kind§r (all its seeds, whatever their genes, each one kept): §l1,024§r items per slot at tier I, §l4,096§r at tier II, §l16,384§r at tier III, §lalways sorted§r:
• §6seed bag§r: 36 / 54 / 72 slots; §lright-click farmland§r: it sows the §lchosen§r seed §l3 × 3§r (Shift: one block), best genes first;
• §6sapling bag§r: 27 / 45 / 63 slots; §lright-click a block§r: it plants the chosen sapling;
• §6fishing creel§r: 36 / 54 / 72 slots of (raw) fish, crustaceans and bait, §lsorted by species§r (the slot keeps the §lbiggest§r size, like the §6wicker basket§r). Fishing catches go §lstraight§r into it; right-click an §laquarium§r: it puts the chosen fish in; a §lcrab pot§r: it baits it; in the §loff hand§r, it gives your rod its bait.
Kept anywhere in the inventory (off hand included), they §lpick up§r their kind by themselves: harvest, fishing, scythe, pots, devices; the first one with room takes it, then the inventory. In the screen: click = one stack, §lright-click = choose§r the kind, Shift + click = to the inventory; buttons §lPickup§r on / off, §lFilter§r (items blocked from pickup), §lStore all§r. §lShift + right-click§r a chest, basket or crate (or the bag in a chest screen): the bag empties into it. §lTiers§r at the crafting grid, the bag in the middle (its contents are kept): II = 4 tanned leather and 2 iron rings, III = 4 coated canvas and 2 steel rings.

At the §lcarpenter's bench§r (saw), in all §l11 woods§r (oak, spruce, birch, jungle, acacia, dark oak, mangrove, cherry, bamboo, crimson, warped), from that wood's planks and laths:
• §6table§r: place several side by side, the top becomes one piece and §llegs only remain at the corners§r;
• §6chair§r: faces you when placed; §lright-click§r to sit down, Shift to stand up;
• §6cabinet§r: §l18 slots§r. On the floor it is a §lcounter§r (worktop, toe kick, doors); with §lnothing below§r (on a wall), a §lwall cupboard§r. Neighboring cabinets join up (no side panel between them, continuous worktop) and turn corners like stairs.
Each piece has the color of §lits own§r wood, everywhere (seat, back, legs). The forge §6tool rack§r also comes in all 11 woods (carpenter: 3 planks, 2 laths, 3 pegs): once placed, it faces you and its 3 tools are seen from the front.

§6Paintings§r now show §lreal works of art§r, all in the public domain: thirty pixel-art reproductions, each at a size that suits it.
• §l1 × 1§r: Van Gogh (Self-Portrait, Sunflowers), Malevich (Black Square), Vermeer (Girl with a Pearl Earring), Mondrian, Dürer (Young Hare), Hokusai (Red Fuji);
• §l1 × 2§r: Wanderer above the Sea of Fog (Friedrich), Mona Lisa (Leonardo da Vinci);
• §l2 × 1§r: Water Lilies, Impression, Sunrise (Monet), The Gleaners (Millet), The Ninth Wave (Aivazovsky), Wheatfield with Crows (Van Gogh);
• §l2 × 2§r: The Kiss (Klimt), Whistler's Mother, The Dance Class (Degas), The Scream (Munch), The Ambassadors (Holbein), Isle of the Dead (Böcklin), The Harvesters (Bruegel), Ejiri (Hokusai), Water Lily Pond (Monet), The Burning of the Houses of Parliament (Turner);
• §l4 × 2§r: The Last Supper; §l4 × 3§r: The Great Wave off Kanagawa, The Birth of Venus; §l4 × 4§r: The Starry Night, Hunters in the Snow, Liberty Leading the People.
A placed painting picks at random among those that fit the wall; in the creative tab, each work has its own item, with its §ltitle§r, §lartist§r and §ldate§r.

§6Map§r (O key): the §lRail§r layer shows tracks (quality color, red: occupied), signals, stations (role color), trains and current deliveries. The Rail tab lists trains, stations, deliveries and alerts (no free train, stuck train, abandoned delivery). Click a train: §lSheet§r (its schedule is set remotely), §lFollow§r (the map keeps it centered), §lSend§r to a station (temporary stop, then it resumes its schedule).
§6PioScript§r: the computer's §lrail§r object:
• rail.trains(), rail.train(name): sheets (name, status, target station, group, speed, vehicles, fuel, mass);
• rail.send(name, station); rail.stations(), rail.station(name) (with the platform stock); rail.set_limit(name, n);
• rail.signal(name, value), rail.get_signal(name): signals read by the §lSignal§r schedule condition;
• rail.weigh(x, y, z): weighbridge reading; rail.deliveries(): the dispatcher's deliveries.
The §lrail.py§r example on the floppy disk closes a station while its platform overflows.

Whatever the order (schedule, send, dispatcher), the ship takes the §lharbor's coordinates§r and works out its route on the water by itself (status “§6Charting a course§r” while it computes, without slowing the game):
• it keeps §l20 blocks off the coasts§r, except in the approach channel of the destination harbor and when leaving its own;
• §lnever explored§r waters are §lsounded§r (the ocean relief is read without loading the world): no need to open the route yourself anymore;
• if land blocks the straight line, it looks for a passage within a 200-block corridor, then 600, then 1,600; beyond that, §6sea beacons§r show the way (next page);
• harbor at the far end of a closed body of water, or no passage found: “§6No feasible sea route§r”, retried every 30 s (at once if the chart or the beacons change). A ship going around in circles gives up too.
On arrival, it waits §6at anchor§r if the berth is taken, then sails into the U-shaped basin; it pushes entities aside and only breaks water plants on its way. The map (O key) shows each ship's remaining route.

Notebook (J), §6Documents§r tab: write documents to a §lplayer§r (picked from the players who have joined) or to a §lclan§r. “+ New”, then the §ltype§r:
• §6Invoice§r: amount lines and a schedule; the recipient pays by hand (reminders, then “overdue”);
• §6Contract§r: signed by both, with or without money; once signed, each installment is §ltaken automatically§r from the recipient; “Real-time contract…” keeps the former hourly contract (page “Contracts and invoices”);
• §6Letter§r: subject and text, delivered to the mailbox;
• §6Quote§r (accepted or refused, nothing is paid), §6Receipt§r (proves a payment, “paid” once sent), §6IOU§r (you pay the recipient, taken automatically), §6Purchase order§r (accepted by the supplier, you pay), §6Bill of sale§r (signed by both, the buyer pays), §6Power of attorney§r (text, accepted, revocable).
§lLetter§r page: recipient, subject (60 characters), text (1,200 characters, 24 lines), signed with your name. §lAmounts§r page: 8 lines (label and amount) and 8 §linstallments§r (in how many game days, amount); “Split” shares the total, one installment every 7 days; §lthe installments must add up to the total§r.
On its day, an installment of a document signed by the payer is taken if they can pay (online with the balance, or the clan treasury); otherwise they are reminded every day (a letter on the first day, then every 7 days) and the document becomes §loverdue§r the next day; “Pay” settles the next installment at any time. The payee can “Forgive” the rest; the author takes back a sent document while nothing is signed or paid. For a clan, a member with the treasury withdrawal right signs and pays. “Print” puts the document on a sheet of paper (former invoices read there too). 40 open documents at most; closed ones go away after 120 game days.

A clan climbs from level 1 to level 10, and never drops back. Its §lpoints§r come from:
• its members' §lproduction§r: 1 point per 100 Shining of value produced, 50 per active member per day at most;
• its §lterritory§r: 2 points per chunk per day, 100 at most;
• its §lfunctions§r: 10 points per Seal placed per day, 80 at most;
• a §lruin restored§r by one of its members: 500, once per ruin.
§lThresholds§r: 500, 1,500, 3,500, 7,000, 12,000, 20,000, 32,000, 50,000, 80,000 points (levels 2 to 10).
Each level opens up members (8, 12, 16… 40), functions and territory budget (+4 chunks).

A player people can §6rally§r villages (§l/faction rally§r, near the bell). It takes:
• a villager village within 200 blocks of a faction chunk;
• a village affinity average of at least §l+60§r toward the federal Representative, and the people of origin at least Esteemed toward the player people;
• §l5,000 Shining§r from the federal treasury, and 3 days of talks shown on the Needs Board.
One rally per 7 days, 6 villages at most. The village takes on the people: its trade, its words and its banners follow the doctrine; names, professions and affinities stay. The player people loses 40 reputation with the people of origin, once.
§lDecline§r: under 2 clans or 12 active members, 14 days to recover; otherwise §ldissolution§r. Rallied villages return to their original people, the name stays reserved for 30 days, and the faction becomes a faction again.

§6Before electricity§r (rotation): hand crank ~5 FE/t equivalent, water wheel ~50, horse mill ~170, windmill ~1,000, beam engine ~630, steam engine ~1,900.
§6MKI → MKIV§r (FE/t, per block):
• dynamo (cap): 2,048 · 8,192 · 32,768 · 131,072;
• solid combustion generator, coal: 2,048 · 8,192 · 32,768 · 131,072;
• liquid combustion generator, diesel: 8,192 · 32,768 · 131,072 · 524,288;
• gas turbine, natural gas: 4,096 · 16,384 · 65,536 · 262,144;
• solar panel (full sun): 512 · 2,048 · 8,192 · 32,768;
• wind turbine, hydro, geothermal (at best): 512 · 2,048 · 8,192 · 32,768;
• microbial fuel cell: 256 · 1,024 · 4,096 · 16,384.
§6First power§r: the dynamo on the steam engine's shaft, then the MKI solid combustion generator (2,048 FE/t, nothing electric in its recipe).
§6Multiblocks§r: steam turbine MKI 51,200 FE/t if the steam keeps up (80 FE per mB); reactors 3.5 to 12.5 MFE/t; solar absorber 2 MFE/t per collector.

Steam that §6drives a steam engine§r (and its dynamo) is used up: nothing comes out. Steam through a §6steam turbine§r (multiblock) comes out as exhaust and must be condensed: MKI §6cooling tower§r 1,000 mB/t (×4 per tier, 95% of the water back) or §6condenser§r (50 to 400 mB/t).
§6Source§r (steam at full power) → MKI towers:
• industrial boiler at full power: MKI 1,920 mB/t → §l2§r; MKII 7,680 → 8 (or 2 MKII towers); MKIII 30,720 → 2 MKIII towers; MKIV 122,880 → 2 MKIV towers;
• fission: Gen I 43,200 → 3 MKIII towers; Gen II 117,000 → 2 MKIV towers; Gen III 48,000 → 3 MKIII towers;
• fusion: tokamak ~76,000 → 2 MKIV towers; heliotron ~20,000 → 2 MKIII towers.
A bronze age boiler feeds the steam engine, not a turbine: its first power is the §ldynamo§r.

In an MKI solid combustion generator (×2 efficiency), one tick of burning is worth 2,048 FE:
• §lcharcoal§r, §lcoal§r: 1,600 ticks, i.e. 3.28 MFE;
• §lcoke§r: 3,200 ticks, 6.55 MFE;
• §lbiochar§r: 1,600 ticks; §lwood pellets§r: 1,200 ticks;
• liquids (liquid combustion generator, ×2 too): §ldiesel§r 16,384 FE per mB, kerosene 14,336, ethanol 6,554;
• gases (gas turbine): §lnatural gas§r 3,277 FE per mB, methane 6,554, biogas 1,092 (multiblock ×1.25).

Before electricity, charcoal heats the forge, the pottery kiln, the smelters and boilers; sawdust and chips burn too. A fuel's tooltip shows its FE.

Every recipe in the mod is a JSON file, editable with datapacks or KubeJS (recipe event, §bevent.custom(...)§r):
• §bpioneers:assembly§r — station (rocket, rover, module, equipment), pattern, key, result, energy, duration, blueprint, research;
• §bpioneers:industrial§r — machine, tier, time, items, fluids, gases, data (type + amount), results, energy (total energy per operation for the Gravity Compressor and the Synthesizer);
• §bpioneers:solar_simulation§r — planet, outputs, duration.

§bconfig/pioneers-common.toml§r (the server decides):
• §levents§r: on/off, calm between two events (40 min by default), chance, each type, flare damage, meteor density, craters, buried meteors, fires, local difficulty;
• §lprotection§r: force field upkeep and impact cost, lightning rod energy per strike;
• §lrockets§r: fuel, transit duration, capsule fuel, landing damage;
• §lsurvival§r: oxygen consumption, suffocation, hostile environment damage;
• §lgas_giants§r: pockets per player, lightning, pressure, drones per bay, drone range and cargo;
• §lendgame§r: absorber power, Space Simulator galaxy energy, cycle time and bonus;
• §lgreenhouse§r, §lmining§r: growth, rover garage range;
• §lrobotics§r: unit power draw, permission to break blocks.

§bconfig/pioneers-mechanics.toml§r: breakage of overloaded parts, source torque, mechanical machine speed, rope range, horse mill feeding; §lvanillaOres§r (ores underground), charcoal in the furnace, charcoal pit speed.

§bconfig/pioneers-client.toml§r: black hole and sun effects, music, camera shake, transit screen, stage separation, event banners and effects.
